{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders","id":1310071,"title":"Custom Wonders","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-05-05T11:26:25-07:00","id":1310071,"title":"Custom Wonders","content":"Even though M20 books (and revised) have some wonders examples, there are lots of effects that require a custom wonder.<br /> Now, the rules presented in M20 says that a wonder can have up to one effect per wonder dot. While the books examples doesn't seem to follow this rule, when choosing a custom wonder at character creation, do you?<br /> From a player perspective, the more effects a wonder has, the better. And paying 6 background points in a 3 dot wonder, or even 10 background points in a very powerful 5 dots wonder and having just a couple of effects seems like you are not having what you paid for.<br /> <br /> Also, I know the highest spehere effect will determine how much dots the wonder requires. But what about the other effects? Couls they be the same sphere dots, for example, a 4 dota wonder having 4 effects of level 4 spheres? Or should they &quot;secondary&quot; effects have lower sphere effects?<br /> <br /> According to the rules, it seems they can all be the same sphere level, equak to the wonder dots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-08T05:15:22-07:00","id":1310723,"title":"","content":"Really? No one buys custom wonders at char creation here?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T05:30:02-07:00","id":1310959,"title":"","content":"Your understanding of the rules it's accurate.<br /> <br /> Perhaps you're baffled by the fact that not only the rules allow you to create sub-optimal wonders, but that most canon wonders <i>are </i>sub-optimal?<br /> <br /> Well, don't be: Mage it's not a wargame and thus Wonders should be built with flavor in mind - that's not a rule but rather the philosophy behind the rules. WoD authors were always against optimization and powergaming, they just don't care about that stile of play. They're more prone to build ceilings than floors, and would take pride on <i>not</i> thinking about efficiency when building a wonder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-09-2019, 08:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T13:27:32-07:00","id":1311045,"title":"","content":"Yes, I am indeed baffled that almost ALL wonders in the books have less than ther rank in number of powers. Right now I can just remember one, that paw that has a lots of powers and turn you into a fight machine.<br /> I know my understanding of the rulesbis correct. What I wanted to know is if other tables tend to create wonders with less powers than tje allowed.<br /> <br /> You have to agree with me that, both in and out-game, one of the objectives of the player/character is to get as powerful as possible. Power is a constant element of the game which can influence directly how things rolls in the history.<br /> <br /> There are rank 5 wonders in books with just one lvl 5 effect. To me it's the same as buy Life 2 butbyou can only use it to heal yourself, nothing more.<br /> This is not powergaming or min/maxing. Is just gettin what you &quot;paid&quot; for.<br /> <br /> But I am not trying to convince anyone or complain. I was just curious to know how you guys go with the wonder creation. When you buy this background at char creation, do you fill all the powers allowed?<br /> <br /> And later i asked the rules about ehat sphere lvls csn every effect be. But seeing examples of wonders with more than 1 power, it seems that you could fill all the effects with the higher rank sphere","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T15:39:30-07:00","id":1311077,"title":"","content":"Well, I usually create wonders with flavour in mind. But I really like the idea that the more powerful an artifact is, the most downsides it's gonna have. Like geas and so on, this way you create more possibilities for the story. In any case, the ST is always the final arbiter and he's gonna decide what he wants of his games, though I think in a more fantastic environment like in the wonderful and brilhant <i>Sorcerers Crusade</i> or the marvelous <i>Dark Ages Mage</i> such things are more approriate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T20:28:42-07:00","id":1311135,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311077#post1311077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I usually create wonders with flavour in mind. But I really like the idea that the more powerful an artifact is, the most downsides it's gonna have. Like geas and so on, this way you create more possibilities for the story. In any case, the ST is always the final arbiter and he's gonna decide what he wants of his games, though I think in a more fantastic environment like in the wonderful and brilhant <i>Sorcerers Crusade</i> or the marvelous <i>Dark Ages Mage</i> such things are more approriate.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Not necessarily. Geas and flaws only decrease the cost of the wonder. You can have a pretty powerfull wonder without any flaw, if you buy its point in background.<br /> And I mean, if you are buying a 10 background points Special Time watch that can stop time (Time 5) when you stop the hands, why not make it able to slow time when you slow the hands movement (Time 3), rewind time by forcing the hands counter-clockwise (Time 3), Speed time when you accelerate the hands (Time 3) and placing a time trigger by setting a timer (Time 4) too?<br /> Flavour is something that is very important and part of the game, but it also is completelly arbitrary. THe player can adapt flavour to his needs in-game, that's not powergaming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T21:25:32-07:00","id":1311140,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311135#post1311135\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Not necessarily. Geas and flaws only decrease the cost of the wonder. You can have a pretty powerfull wonder without any flaw, if you buy its point in background.<br /> And I mean, if you are buying a 10 background points Special Time watch that can stop time (Time 5) when you stop the hands, why not make it able to slow time when you slow the hands movement (Time 3), rewind time by forcing the hands counter-clockwise (Time 3), Speed time when you accelerate the hands (Time 3) and placing a time trigger by setting a timer (Time 4) too?<br /> Flavour is something that is very important and part of the game, but it also is completelly arbitrary. THe player can adapt flavour to his needs in-game, that's not powergaming.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, sure! Then why not have a ring that controls the mind of anyone in your line of sight, any number of people at the same time and then, why not, anyone anywhere in the world? Surely it's &quot;not powergaming&quot;! LOL!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T22:22:47-07:00","id":1311155,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311140#post1311140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Yes, sure! Then why not have a ring that controls the mind of anyone in your line of sight, any number of people at the same time and then, why not, anyone anywhere in the world? Surely it's &quot;not powergaming&quot;! LOL!</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well, an effect like that would require tons of successes and the target always can resist mind control. You are describing a Mind 4 Correspondence 4 wonder that would cost 8 background dots and have Arete 4. With only this 4 dice you'd have to mind control hundreds of people and to make this anywhere in the world would probably require waaaay more successes than 4 dice can. I don't want to look at the books right now to give you exaclty how much successes are needed, but I can assure you that you would require more than 10.<br /> Trying to use exagerated examples to invalidate my argument is not going to work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-09T22:31:10-07:00","id":1311158,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311155#post1311155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Well, an effect like that would require tons of successes and the target always can resist mind control. You are describing a Mind 4 Correspondence 4 wonder that would cost 8 background dots and have Arete 4. With only this 4 dice you'd have to mind control hundreds of people and to make this anywhere in the world would probably require waaaay more successes than 4 dice can. I don't want to look at the books right now to give you exaclty how much successes are needed, but I can assure you that you would require more than 10.<br /> Trying to use exagerated examples to invalidate my argument is not going to work.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hey chill out man. It was just a joke. As I said before everything depends on the kind of game the Storyteller wants to roll. Some people prefer more &quot;down to earth&quot; games and others go the more high fantasy route. But in my opinion Sorcerers Crusade is more appropriate for this kind of high fantasy environment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T01:22:56-07:00","id":1311176,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1310959#post1310959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, don't be: Mage it's not a wargame and thus Wonders should be built with flavor in mind - that's not a rule but rather the philosophy behind the rules. WoD authors were always against optimization and powergaming, they just don't care about that stile of play. They're more prone to build ceilings than floors, and would take pride on <i>not</i> thinking about efficiency when building a wonder.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love this post so very, VERY much. I want to hug you.<br /> <br /> Whenever I write character backgrounds, wonders, rotes or what-have-you, I try to approach it from a simple perspective: &quot;Would this thing/person/whatever be really interesting, fun and/or intriguing in a TV series/movie/novel, or would it be bland and too simple?&quot;<br /> It's a good guideline to actually make flavorful things, and even better: It's *fun* to come up with creative things along those lines.<br /> <br /> Min/Maxing is boring. Take your points, squeeze in as much as you can, minimize any actual flaws and downsides, and then think of any actual in-game reasonings after. It makes everybody and everything more of the same, almost as if the characters themselves read the rulebook, until you get the RPG variant of a minmax Metabuild.<br /> It's fine in videogames where it can at times turn into an art to optimize builds, but in anything that's supposed to be collaborative storytelling (or one-sided storytelling in the case of pasively watched/read media), it starts getting oh so one-dimensional.<br /> <br /> It's the same reason I always get wary whenever discussions about maxing all-encompassing damages/protections/what-have-you begins. My thought always goes back to &quot;Yes, but is that character even the kind of person to do that? Is that kind of thing even part of their personality? Does their belief and practice even teach such things?&quot;<br /> I guess it's the old make-most-use-of-game-mechanics vs. Roleplaying (capital R) conflict.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 05-10-2019, 04:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T01:27:43-07:00","id":1311178,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311176#post1311176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love this post so very, VERY much. I want to hug you.<br /> <br /> Whenever I write character backgrounds, wonders, rotes or what-have-you, I try to approach it from a simple perspective: &quot;Would this thing/person/whatever be really interesting, fun and/or intriguing in a TV series/movie/novel, or would it be bland and too simple?&quot;<br /> It's a good guideline to actually make flavorful things, and even better: It's *fun* to come up with creative things along those lines.<br /> <br /> Min/Maxing is boring. Take your points, squeeze in as much as you can, minimize any actual flaws and downsides, and then think of any actual in-game reasonings after. It makes everybody and everything more of the same, until you get the RPG variant of a minmax Metabuild.<br /> It's fine in videogames where it can at times turn into an art to optimize builds, but in anything that's supposed to be a collaborative storytelling (or one-sided storytelling in the case of pasively watched/read media), it starts getting oh so one-dimensional.<br /> <br /> It's the same reason I always get wary whenever discussions about maxing all-encompassing damages/protections/what-have-you begins. My thought always goes back to &quot;Yes, but is that character even the kind of person to do that? Is that kind of thing even part of their personality? Does their belief and practice even teach such things?&quot;<br /> I guess it's the old make-most-use-of-game-mechanics vs. Roleplaying (capital R) conflict.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Beautiful! That's exactly my thought! I try to see the things that happen in my game as if I was reading a book, this is a great guideline to make things interesting in the game and not only min/maxing that eventually makes everything so shalow! We need drama! Not super powers!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T06:10:57-07:00","id":1311203,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6606_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6606\" data-vbnamecard=\"6606\">Ambrosia</a> what does that suppose to mean? You think that choosing to have more powers in a pretty powerful wonder is min/maxing?<br /> The wonder can have its fluff, fittting into the character history and all the roleplaying cool aspect and still be able to possess more than 1 power.<br /> <br /> I gave the example of a watch that controls Time. And I can't think of a difference, flavour wise, if it have just the lvl 5 effect or all o the effects I said.<br /> <br /> Having said that, does your response means that you tend to get just 1 power (or seldom fully buying the wonder potential) in your characters creation?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T07:23:01-07:00","id":1311211,"title":"","content":"Ah, the age old false dichotomy of optimization vs narrative attractiveness.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T07:38:23-07:00","id":1311216,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6606_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6606\" data-vbnamecard=\"6606\">Ambrosia</a> I'm pretty sure htat IS the style WoD authors had in mind when writting the books, yes.<br /> <br /> Probably <i>Lord Revan</i> plays a different style, which it's OK, but requires to understand that one may find even more holes in the rules, and &quot;traps&quot; within the book, than other players do. Simply because WoD authors hadn't that style's mindset when they wrote the books<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You have to agree with me that, both in and out-game, one of the objectives of the player<b>/</b>character is to get as powerful as possible. Power is a constant element of the game which can influence directly how things rolls in the history.</div> </div> </div> </div>While my characters tend to appreciate power (I like to play wizardly wizards, and mad geniuses), I don't play with an ST that's too harsh with the rules and encounters (not when I play WoD) nor with competitive PvP players. Hence I don't fell compelled, as a player, to get as much as I can.<br /> <br /> I know that I'm going to have a chance of doing whatever my characters want to do. Even if it requires bending the rules a little, because we play with the Rule of Cool well in mind.<br /> <br /> I also know (due to the same narrative) that<i> no matter how many dots I have in my chart</i>, the ST it's always going to create treaths that will overwhelm and endanger my character.<br /> <br /> Point is: The people that I play with won't put as much value on dots or rules (when we play WoD), hence <i>I know <u>for certain</u></i> that while these may be useful to some extent, they won't be decisive<br /> <br /> That's why I, or the books for the matter, don't have to agree. If I played WoD as a wargame, with an ST that carefully balances &quot;encounters&quot; having an efficient use of the rules in mind, then I would have to agree because getting a 5th power for my 5th level wonder may well be the difference between being helpful or not when the party gets overrun by vampires. It's a matter of playing style. <hr />Having said that, my answer to your concrete question would be: It depends. I don't care either way<br /> <br /> I don't think anything should be banned just because it's powerful, but still I wouldn't make wonders like you make them.<br /> <br /> For instance, the Time Watch:<br /> <br /> If I were to make a watch that includes the Time 5 effect to step out of time, and the fluff it's that the time outside moves according to the hands and only the user it's &quot;outside&quot; that (allowing me to move when the hands are still), I would never add an effect to slow time <i>that would affect someone else</i>, or a Time 4<i> that would affect a spell. </i>Those would be against the flavor of the watch as I envision it.<br /> <br /> I would include a power to Rewind time when the hands are forced counter-clockwise. And I would absolutely add to that a Time 1 effect so the watch it's always on time (after all, it's a watch that rewinds time when it's hands are forced counter-clockwise - it makes sense for it to be so connected with the flow of time that it's <i>always</i> <i>in time</i>. If you're sent to the future, the date will be correct and the hands will move accordingly).<br /> <br /> I <i>might </i>add an effect to ascelerate time, but it would ascelerate the time <i>outside</i>, not the time of the user (no Celerity) - not sure how that can be useful, but it surely goes hand to hand with rewinding when the hands move counter-clockwise and keeping time still when the hands are still<br /> <br /> And that's it, the Wonder it's powerful enough for my tastes with those 4 effects, even if the 3rd and 4th aren't terribly useful (really, the lvl 5 one it's broken by itself).<br /> <br /> Of course, what you find flavorful may vary. It may very well be that 5 powers don't represent all that the Wonder should do in your imagination, but the point it's that: No, I don't feel compelled to put 5 powers or 1 power. That consideration only guides my judgement in the sense that I can't have more than 5 because rules says so, and I *can* have more than 1 if I fell like it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-10-2019, 11:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T08:32:58-07:00","id":1311227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311211#post1311211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, the age old false dichotomy of optimization vs narrative attractiveness.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is not a 'vs' at all. IMO. Narrative atractiveness and Optimization are no opposing rivals. They can well co-exist and one can come from the other.<br /> But, narrative attractiveness should not harshly suffer under optimization; So, in that regard it is more a weighting of the two rather than actual opposition. When a character makes decisions strongly based on OOC mechanics rather than in-world events or their personal past and experiences, for example, is when the scales get tipped too much.<br /> Likewise when a character or item clearly is built with the mechanics first, and any story or in-game reason for doing so second instead of the other way around - at least in my opinion.<br /> As Aleph said, to each their own, and there might be game table where a sort of 'story second' powergaming might be necessary...but it sounds rather strange to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> To add, it also boils down to things and characters with flaws and reasonable abilities instead of Overpower ('abilities' depending on context..) being just more interesting to observe and, IMO, to act out.<br /> Now, there are always exceptions to this; My absolute favorite example is the series &quot;One Punch Man&quot;: <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">If you'd have asked me at one point in the past &quot;Hey, can a series about an invincible guy who can destroy any enemy in a single punch be entertaining?&quot; I'd have gone &quot;Lolno.&quot;<br /> But it is an utterly entertaining and fun watch; Not because the character lacks RAW POWAH, but because he is just adorably ignorant when it comes to social contexts, that he just ends up in absolutely bizarre situations. It works excellently as a comedy series. If the series actually was a *serious* one, with this kind of character at its center, however, it would not work.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 05-10-2019, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T09:01:18-07:00","id":1311232,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311203#post1311203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6606_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6606\" data-vbnamecard=\"6606\">Ambrosia</a> what does that suppose to mean? You think that choosing to have more powers in a pretty powerful wonder is min/maxing?<br /> The wonder can have its fluff, fittting into the character history and all the roleplaying cool aspect and still be able to possess more than 1 power.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not at all! There can be good reasons for a wonder having more than one effect on it. That's no the issue at all. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> But, a wonder should not have more effects simply because &quot;Else it would not have as many effects as its points allow.&quot;. I mean, it can be the reason I guess, but I don't think it should be.<br /> <br /> Instead the reason should be (Mostly - we can't really completely push away OOC influences after all..) based on the logical decisions of a character, and based on that character's history and current situation. Under the premise that the character does not know about any points, but instead knows 'More powerful wonders can be made, but it's harder and more risky'.<br /> <br /> We don't know the reason why most example Wonders stick to minimal effects. I mean, there are some wonders that have multiple; I highly suggest getting the &quot;Mystical Armory&quot; book on the Storyteller's Vault. It lists all wonders from all the Mage books written <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> (And also get &quot;Enlightened Grimoire&quot;! It is by the same author, just collecting all Rotes from all books).<br /> <br /> For example, the Technocracy's NWO might have decided to only put one or two effects on their NWO Tru-Mirrorshades simply because they work under the Unix-tool principle of &quot;It does only one job, but it does it well.&quot;. It would fit the NWO's paranoia well to not make tools prone to failure-due-to-complexity. Or perhaps glasses with that scope of few effects the Technocracy's timetable well - making them easier to mass-produce into the Consensus later - and the NWO got told to keep their abilities minimal.<br /> <br /> Things like that <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> On the Tradition's side, the reason for a Wonder's set of effects are much more personal - starting from a lack of a character's in-game creatitivty and ending in some subgroups perhaps actually stressing philosophies that teach importance of 'pure' Wonders with clear, single intent. I can see that as being a very Zen thing. I'm looking at you, Akashics.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 05-10-2019, 12:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T11:14:54-07:00","id":1311250,"title":"","content":"It seems that indeed this will vary from table to table. Getting more power, having more options to do in-game is really fun. Even min/maxing can be fun. D&amp;D have strong use of this practice and it still very fun to play.<br /> <br /> But, my point is that you can have both. You can have the fluff and still want the best of your character. And as Aleph said, the ST will give challenges no matter how powerful your mage is. The only difference is that with more powers, the mage tend to have more options to deal with the challenges. <br /> If power was inverse proportionality to fun, just play a mortal and have the most fun. <br /> <br /> Again, you can build the flavour of your wonder and try to get the most of it whitout ruining the roleplay.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a>, you made a good analysis of the example I gave. What if instead of a watch, it's a hourglass. Thst you still manipi time by rotating it and changing the flow of the sand (or no flow at all at horizontal state to stop time). And the mage can get a bit of sand off the glass o throw at someone/something to slow it. And when the mage cast a spell using the sand or maybe just cast the spell on the sandglass first, the tine trigger is activated?<br /> <br /> Since the fluff is up to the player, and the player want to have cool powers, why not get the bes of both worlds? I could give others examples that even use different spheres.<br /> <br /> Now, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> said that wouldn't put those effects (slow and trigger) into the watch. Why is that? In game a wonder is made by a powerful mage that can cast lots of effects. The mage will decide the full characteristics of the wonder and have only the limits of number of spheres and arete a wonder can sustain, that is determined by the rules. <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6606_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6606\" data-vbnamecard=\"6606\">Ambrosia</a> posted some reasons involving mass production and consensus inclusion. While that makes sense by a Technocrat view, i can't see a mystic worrying about those things.<br /> Sorry, but a mage with Arete 5 and master of spheres lacking creativity to the point of giving only 1 effect toca lvl5 wonder is just silly. And why wouldn't a wonder be impure if more than 1 effect is infused?<br /> <br /> To me, looks like the effort to explain not having more powers is going more agains the flavour than putting all the powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T12:15:54-07:00","id":1311262,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311250#post1311250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, Aleph said that wouldn't put those effects (slow and trigger) into the watch. Why is that? In game a wonder is made by a powerful mage that can cast lots of effects. The mage will decide the full characteristics of the wonder and have only the limits of number of spheres and arete a wonder can sustain, that is determined by the rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't like to mix Systems and Fiction. The only notion a mage has about Spheres and Arete it's as a rather cumbersome &quot;magical esperanto&quot; used to explain stuff to mages of other Traditions - only Hermetics use &quot;Spheres&quot;. More so, a mage doesn't know how many effects can go at what number of successes or all that mechanical stuff. Mechanics are used to abstract and symplify what <i>really </i>happens in game, not to <i>define </i>it.<br /> <br /> In my example I did the fluff reasons of why the watch does what it does. It's a watch that's linked with time, manipulates it, and immunizes the user against the change. The watch affects the relationship between the mage and the flow of time time. In a bizarre sense it's a &quot;single&quot; magick what's at work, but one that has many moving parts (and thus the mechanic represents it a several effects).<br /> <br /> Affecting a single or a few enemies, or even a small area, like a &quot;slowing bomb&quot; or &quot;slowing ray&quot; wouldn't jive well with the idea. And about the triggering, it's because it's affecting a spell rather than time itself. You could sell those effects to me if you made them more in tune with the general idea behind the watch<br /> <br /> In the case of the sand clock. I get changing the flow of the sand, but it really sounds like the sand it's a Charm, since you take some and throw it - if it's magick sand I guess it get's expended eventually. It really sound like two Wonders to me (nothing says you can't have a Wonder inside another, but they're still two). Other than that I see no advantage over a mechanical clock.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-10-2019, 03:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-10T14:10:08-07:00","id":1311283,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> I disagree. While the mage doesn't speak as spheres he certainly knows the limitations and how to imbue effects into it. Actually, in game it should even be more complex than out of game. The rules work to give the player a simpmer description of what the mage does. But in game is up o the mage to decide exactly how the wonder works, having no limits other than his paradigm, his knowledge of magick (Arete,sphere) and the ammount of magick the object imbued can hold. <br /> So, no good reason to stop at 1 power.<br /> <br /> The hourglass could be the wonder and any sand putted inside would do for the effects. It has the advantage of being something that can affect external targets, no just time itself. But that's just an example. <br /> <br /> My point is, why instead of buying a lvl 3 wonder described in the book with just one lvl3 effect, not take the same (or very similar) wonder with some other cool (and flavoured) effects?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-12T06:47:30-07:00","id":1311550,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311227#post1311227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is not a 'vs' at all. IMO. Narrative atractiveness and Optimization are no opposing rivals. They can well co-exist and one can come from the other.<br /> But, narrative attractiveness should not harshly suffer under optimization; So, in that regard it is more a weighting of the two rather than actual opposition. When a character makes decisions strongly based on OOC mechanics rather than in-world events or their personal past and experiences, for example, is when the scales get tipped too much.<br /> Likewise when a character or item clearly is built with the mechanics first, and any story or in-game reason for doing so second instead of the other way around - at least in my opinion.<br /> As Aleph said, to each their own, and there might be game table where a sort of 'story second' powergaming might be necessary...but it sounds rather strange to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> To add, it also boils down to things and characters with flaws and reasonable abilities instead of Overpower ('abilities' depending on context..) being just more interesting to observe and, IMO, to act out.<br /> Now, there are always exceptions to this; My absolute favorite example is the series &quot;One Punch Man&quot;: <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">If you'd have asked me at one point in the past &quot;Hey, can a series about an invincible guy who can destroy any enemy in a single punch be entertaining?&quot; I'd have gone &quot;Lolno.&quot;<br /> But it is an utterly entertaining and fun watch; Not because the character lacks RAW POWAH, but because he is just adorably ignorant when it comes to social contexts, that he just ends up in absolutely bizarre situations. It works excellently as a comedy series. If the series actually was a *serious* one, with this kind of character at its center, however, it would not work.</div></div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>You brought up a &quot;narrative first perspective&quot; as in direct accordance to not caring for optimization, painting the two things as a dichotomy, or somehow mutually exclusive.<br /> Your reply to me contradicts your own words.<br /> <br /> But if you do agree with me, that there's nothing stopping anyone from building both an interesting and a powerful character, and the people who do not do that are acting out of their own biases, there's no need to continue this particular discussion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":32,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","user":"Synapse","date":". 05-12-2019, 09:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-13T05:54:29-07:00","id":1311760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I disagree. While the mage doesn't speak as spheres he certainly knows the limitations and how to imbue effects into it. Actually, in game it should even be more complex than out of game. The rules work to give the player a simpmer description of what the mage does. But in game is up o the mage to decide exactly how the wonder works, having no limits other than his paradigm, his knowledge of magick (Arete,sphere) and the ammount of magick the object imbued can hold.<br /> So, no good reason to stop at 1 power. </div> </div> </div>Indeed we do disagree: My understanding about the meaning of simplicity it's exactly the opposite of yours<br /> <br /> With simple rules it's easy to understand the limitations because there's few of them. Simple rules are bound to be <i>vague</i> and thus have fewer and very general restrictions. More complex rules that take in count the intricacies of Paradigm would have a lot more &quot;factors&quot; stepping in, making very hard to know the exact limits of wonders.<br /> <br /> \u200bIf you think about that, it's <i>absurd </i>that the rules of wonders should be the same for everyone (or even for every <i>kind </i>of Wonder) in a world where what Magick can do it's determined by Belief. If that was the case then there's no way in hell mages would be so stupid as to not recognize those simple rules as the universal truth.<br /> <br /> Since we have cleared that: Precisely because the rules are a vague abstraction of a complex process, there's no reason to think that,<i> in game</i>, a mage can always add 5 effects to a 5 level Wonder - that's just a generalization that doesn't take in count how the mage <i>believes </i>Wonder-making works.<br /> <br /> So, let's sumarize why I think you're wrong:<br /> <br /> a) Belief it's entirely under the control of the player, and just like the player can decide the mage isn't a combat murder machine regardless of the wishes of the mages, he can say that the mage just doesn't have a efficient belief<br /> <br /> b) If mages cared IOC about efficiency more than about belief, they would scorn mages that have cumbersome Instruments, like altars, sex, labs, external focuses in general and those that are hard to carry around in particular. You know, the stuff we've seen you always want to avoid in your threads. Yet they don't seem to care much about that in the fiction.<br /> <br /> c)If they don't know about Spheres, they don't know about the rest of the rules that depend on that system either. Once we have sepparated the system from the world, it's disingenuous to think that it's easy or even possible to sistematize how magick works - if anything that should be a function of Belief, because fluff wise magick comes from belief.<br /> <br /> And since, in the fiction, we see that mages make Wonders with only one effect more often than not, then the only reasonable conclusion it's that Canon mages don't have efficient Beliefs. Not even the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> d) As an example, in the case of the Time Watch: The watch does <u>one thing</u> and only <u>one thing</u>, the magictech involved in making the watch it's all devoted to that and my mage has to have a good idea of how much effort it takes to make that magitech, however that the rules abstract it into &quot;four effects&quot; it's something my mage can't possibly know IOC.<br /> <br /> Since a mage can't know that the Wonder has <i>four effects</i>, how can he know that the Wonder can have one more?.<br /> <br /> I think it would be very <i>convinient </i>that the &quot;complex laws&quot; of your Focus allows to determine exactly what an effect costs, after we have dealt with the fact that your mage doesn't know about the rules per se (it sounds like a way to avoid the issue).<br /> <br /> If we are going to invent that magick as mages understand it has a formalization other than the rules <i>we know mages don't know</i>, such understanding has to be adjusted to the Lore of the game - and the Lore doesn't indicate that mages make the most efficient use of the OOC rules, if anything it indicates the contrary (thus either their understanding of the laws of magick it's very wrong, or the additional &quot;complexity&quot; you speak about it's not very enabling).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The hourglass could be the wonder and any sand putted inside would do for the effects. It has the advantage of being something that can affect external targets, no just time itself. But that's just an example. </div> </div> </div>See, this is an amazing example of erasing flavour in favor of powergaming.<br /> <br /> Any sand putted inside would do for the effects?, how so?. Because normaly sand doesn't freeze people in time...the hourglass &quot;enchants&quot; the sand to give it the property to freeze people?: OK, that sounds Talisman/Artifact that creates Charms. Feasible, but costly in terms of Quintessence.<br /> <br /> But no, I know where you're going to: You don't want the sand to be special in any way, you just toss it because you feel that pointing the clock to someon and willing to paralize like a magic wand would be too blatantly outside the flavor of a hourglass, as it has nothing to do with the movement of the sand, a measure of time, or anything.<br /> <br /> It really sounds like an excuse, and it's not working because there's nothing in a hourglass that alters time according to the movement of the sand that indicates that tossing the content outside would do precisely what you want - the sand it's not part of the glass anymore, why should it do anything if the hourglass doesn't affect it in any way and can't control it anymore?.<br /> <br /> And that's exactly what I and Ambrosia are criticizing. You're not thinking on the flavour of the Wonder at all. You're not even <i>trying </i>to explain how that works within the <i>flavor </i>of the hourglass.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311283#post1311283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My point is, why instead of buying a lvl 3 wonder described in the book with just one lvl3 effect, not take the same (or very similar) wonder with some other cool (and flavoured) effects?</div> </div> </div> </div>It just doesn't come naturally. I could have tought on a 5th flavorfull power to add to my Time Watch, but nothing came to my mind at the moment of creating the wonder and I won't loose my sleep nor ask the ST to retcon my build just because of that. If I suddenly think on a power that my Time Watch &quot;must have&quot;, well, it's time for a Mk2 Time Watch.<br /> <br /> To put it simply:<br /> <br /> OOC, If the Wonder it's flavorfull with one power, there's just no good reason to add more. And IOC mages don't seem to know about that because they don't do it (perhaps because IOC there's no &quot;Effects&quot;, &quot;Spheres&quot; or &quot;Successes&quot; to talk about how many of those can be devoted into a Wonder, and magick it's so complex that those limits can't be fixed in any reliable fashion)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-13-2019, 09:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-15T13:24:13-07:00","id":1312392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Indeed we do disagree: My understanding about the meaning of simplicity it's exactly the opposite of yours<br /> <br /> With simple rules it's easy to understand the limitations because there's few of them. Simple rules are bound to be <i>vague</i> and thus have fewer and very general restrictions. More complex rules that take in count the intricacies of Paradigm would have a lot more &quot;factors&quot; stepping in, making very hard to know the exact limits of wonders.<br /> <br /> ?If you think about that, it's <i>absurd </i>that the rules of wonders should be the same for everyone (or even for every <i>kind </i>of Wonder) in a world where what Magick can do it's determined by Belief. If that was the case then there's no way in hell mages would be so stupid as to not recognize those simple rules as the universal truth.</div> </div> </div> </div>We agree partially. I said that the game's rules is a simpler way to describe a complex in-game process of wonder creation. Note that I said process, not rule. The wonders do have an specific limitation according to paradigm. It is said over and over in the books that a mage cannot use, and thus create, a wonder that does not fit his paradigm. The rules of creation out of game should be the same for any paradigm. In-game they really aren't but the differences are represented in flavor. While a technoract might use a factory or laboratory to create his wonders, a witch could use a caldroun or other mystic way.<br /> <br /> Another very important thing about Mage is that the ultimate truth is that magick works because of mage's Will. So, while they might use different methods to focus, and the majority doesn't even consider that's just a way to focus, but how magick really works, their spells follow the same &quot;universal rule&quot; (purple paradigm). The same applies to wonders. While they might be created by different forms, the process of creation and imbuing power to the object is the same for technocrats or mystics. Afterall, it is basically crafting the item, casting the spells (whith focus varying according to paradigm) and using Quintessence.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since we have cleared that: Precisely because the rules are a vague abstraction of a complex process, there's no reason to think that,<i> in game</i>, a mage can always add 5 effects to a 5 level Wonder - that's just a generalization that doesn't take in count how the mage <i>believes </i>Wonder-making works.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, if the Mage paradigm allows him to cast the effects that are going to be in the wonder, and the wonder is an object that also is part of that paradigm, then there is no reason to say that the mage cannot put 5 effects into the wonder. Those are the only restrictions about wonders. If you have the knowledge (spheres/arete), the material (quintessence/object) and a fitting paradigm, then you can create it.<br /> He can do that because this is how the rules of creation are described in Mage. The differences of mages creating different wonders lays in their focus and their necessary practices/instruments. If the mage wants 3 effects he just have to cast the 3 effects in the process of creation, considering he meets the other requisites (Spheres/Arete/skills/Quintessence). <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, let's sumarize why I think you're wrong:<br /> <br /> a) Belief it's entirely under the control of the player, and just like the player can decide the mage isn't a combat murder machine regardless of the wishes of the mages, he can say that the mage just doesn't have a efficient belief</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, a player can choose to be less powerfull than he could. I just want to understand why.<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar32_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"32\" data-vbnamecard=\"32\">Synapse</a> said, there is no duality between power and an interesting character. In fact, lots of players find powerfull characters more fun to play. If there was, why don't you play human mortal without powers and 7 flaws and throw away your freebies? It'll be a lot of fun, no power at all.<br /> Also, power doesn't always equal combat efficiency. Having more options (more spheres levels, more wonders, or whatever) means having more ways to deal with problems that your mage is going to find.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">b) If mages cared IOC about efficiency more than about belief, they would scorn mages that have cumbersome Instruments, like altars, sex, labs, external focuses in general and those that are hard to carry around in particular. You know, the stuff we've seen you always want to avoid in your threads. Yet they don't seem to care much about that in the fiction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I am a very practical guy and I reflect this on my char. Mages do care about efficiency, but, unlike the players, they cannot chose what they indeed can do or cannot. An akashic might be able to cast an effect just by using hand mudras, while the hermetic would need an altar,symbols and long chants. That's because their individual story, their beliefs and how they learned how to do magick. The hermetic would certainly prefer to cast his spell without all this complex instruments, but he just can't or doesn't think it would work without it. They care so much that they drop those very instrument as soon as they reach the power level (Arete) to do it.<br /> Of course, you could make a mage that doens't want power, doesn't spend xp in sphere or arete and need to recite the entire bible to cast the simplest effects. That's up to you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">c)If they don't know about Spheres, they don't know about the rest of the rules that depend on that system either. Once we have sepparated the system from the world, it's disingenuous to think that it's easy or even possible to sistematize how magick works - if anything that should be a function of Belief, because fluff wise magick comes from belief.</div> </div> </div> </div>The character do not know the rules of Mage: The Ascension, but he might know how much spells he can put into an object, and how much Quintessence and effort he will need to put itto work.<br /> The process will be as complex as the mage's paradigm/focus is. M20 Book of Secrets had a very detailed description of wonder creation and it clear that to put more than 1 effect in a wonder (that has more than rank 1), they just need to castballbthe effects on the object as part of the process of creation. <br /> You could house-rule and say that the mage wouldn't know how much effects the wonder can support, but a Awarness or even Prime roll would answer that. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And since, in the fiction, we see that mages make Wonders with only one effect more often than not, then the only reasonable conclusion it's that Canon mages don't have efficient Beliefs. Not even the Technocracy.</div> </div> </div> </div>What you mean by inefficient belief? As I see any belief could create a wonder with 4 effects. As long as it fits the paradigm. The kind of wonder, not the number of effects is limited by belief.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">d) As an example, in the case of the Time Watch: The watch does <u>one thing</u> and only <u>one thing</u>, the magictech involved in making the watch it's all devoted to that and my mage has to have a good idea of how much effort it takes to make that magitech, however that the rules abstract it into &quot;four effects&quot; it's something my mage can't possibly know IOC.<br /> <br /> Since a mage can't know that the Wonder has <i>four effects</i>, how can he know that the Wonder can have one more?.<br /> <br /> I think it would be very <i>convinient </i>that the &quot;complex laws&quot; of your Focus allows to determine exactly what an effect costs, after we have dealt with the fact that your mage doesn't know about the rules per se (it sounds like a way to avoid the issue).</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, the mage might not know about sphere and arete points, but he know about quintessence,tass and individual effects. He also could know how much spells he can put in a wonder before it blows, for example. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If we are going to invent that magick as mages understand it has a formalization other than the rules <i>we know mages don't know</i>, such understanding has to be adjusted to the Lore of the game - and the Lore doesn't indicate that mages make the most efficient use of the OOC rules, if anything it indicates the contrary (thus either their understanding of the laws of magick it's very wrong, or the additional &quot;complexity&quot; you speak about it's not very enabling).</div> </div> </div> </div>What your point here? I already covered that when talked about how mages doesn't choose what they believe or can do. Also, the complexity is reflected in paradigm and focus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1311760#post1311760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, this is an amazing example of erasing flavour in favor of powergaming.<br /> <br /> Any sand putted inside would do for the effects?, how so?. Because normaly sand doesn't freeze people in time...the hourglass &quot;enchants&quot; the sand to give it the property to freeze people?: OK, that sounds Talisman/Artifact that creates Charms. Feasible, but costly in terms of Quintessence.<br /> <br /> But no, I know where you're going to: You don't want the sand to be special in any way, you just toss it because you feel that pointing the clock to someon and willing to paralize like a magic wand would be too blatantly outside the flavor of a hourglass, as it has nothing to do with the movement of the sand, a measure of time, or anything.<br /> <br /> It really sounds like an excuse, and it's not working because there's nothing in a hourglass that alters time according to the movement of the sand that indicates that tossing the content outside would do precisely what you want - the sand it's not part of the glass anymore, why should it do anything if the hourglass doesn't affect it in any way and can't control it anymore?.<br /> <br /> And that's exactly what I and Ambrosia are criticizing. You're not thinking on the flavour of the Wonder at all. You're not even <i>trying </i>to explain how that works within the <i>flavor </i>of the hourglass.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It just doesn't come naturally. I could have tought on a 5th flavorfull power to add to my Time Watch, but nothing came to my mind at the moment of creating the wonder and I won't loose my sleep nor ask the ST to retcon my build just because of that. If I suddenly think on a power that my Time Watch &quot;must have&quot;, well, it's time for a Mk2 Time Watch.<br /> <br /> To put it simply:<br /> <br /> OOC, If the Wonder it's flavorfull with one power, there's just no good reason to add more. And IOC mages don't seem to know about that because they don't do it (perhaps because IOC there's no &quot;Effects&quot;, &quot;Spheres&quot; or &quot;Successes&quot; to talk about how many of those can be devoted into a Wonder, and magick it's so complex that those limits can't be fixed in any reliable fashion)</div> </div> </div> </div>There is no relation of wonder object and effect. You might have a ring that open portals in the space wherever the mage wants like the ones used in Dr.Strange. You could have a wand where you point and shouts &quot;blow this motherfucker!&quot; and a ball of fire comes out.<br /> We see devices in movies/series where they point it to a thing and a ray comes out to paralize/teleport/melt it.<br /> This is a magick items, for fcks sake! Anything is possible. If you don't want to throw sand you might just get the Time Watch and wave it at someone saying &quot;hocus fokus, slow this motherfucker&quot; and make them slow.<br /> Combining the object with the effect might be good for coincidental effects (a plasma shooting gun) and fitting into the consensus. But that is not a limitation. We see crazy magick items everywhere.<br /> And no, this is not powergaming. You even paid for the wonder with background points.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-15T18:31:43-07:00","id":1312465,"title":"","content":"The Wonder was created by way of a Focus (what earlier editions called a paradigm), which includes a Practice that defines the mechanics of magic as understood by the crafter. As such, it works in a manner consistent with that Focus. The resulting Wonder may seem crazy to a Sleeper; but that doesn't mean that there's no rhyme or reason to it \u2014 it's just that the rhyme and reason isn't that of the Consensus. <br /> <br /> If I'm understanding Aleph's point correctly, it's that you're not concerning yourself with the magical beliefs of the Wonder's creator: it needs to work in a way that makes sense to <i>him</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-15T19:33:09-07:00","id":1312474,"title":"","content":"My gateway into roleplaying was Champions. The second roleplaying system I ever got into was GURPS. At the time (the mid-80s), these games were innovative for their point-accounting systems. In particular, GURPS was built on the notion that you could make any character you wanted to, given a sufficient point budget. <br /> <br /> There was only one small problem with that, and it took me a while to recognize it: when character creation is an exercise in point accounting, you tend to make your choices based on what's optimal in the point accounting scheme, not necessarily what suits your character concept. Hero System, which grew out of Champions, is rather notorious for this mindset, which is generally referred to as min-maxing: trying to squeeze every last bit of efficiency out of the game's accounting system with only a token regard for concept. The latter was generally tacked on after the fact as little more than an afterthought. <br /> <br /> I'm getting a lot of that vibe from this thread: an emphasis of thinking in terms of what the game system will allow you to do and then wrestling every last bit of advantage out of it that you can, with the in-setting rationale being treated as a post hoc \u201cI guess I have to do this\u201d sort of thing rather than being the heart of the design: a coat of paint, rather than the engine. <br /> <br /> In my games, Concept is King. And that works both ways: first, if the concept for a Wonder only includes one or two Effects, then the Wonder only has one or two Effects. Second, if the concept for the Wonder justifies a half-dozen Effects, then the Wonder gets a half-dozen Effects. For me, the \u201cfive Effects per Wonder\u201d rule isn't so much a rule as it is a guideline: it's more a matter of \u201cfew Wonders will have as many as five Effects; fewer still will have more\u201d. If a player presents to me a concept for a Wonder that breaks the five Effects rule, and the concept is a good one, then I'm going to bend the rules and let him have it. <br /> <br /> Not that that particular issue has ever come up, mind you; it's been my experience that once you break free of the min-maxer mindset and stop viewing the rules as limits hedging you in and boundaries to be pushed against, you find that you have no problem settling for less than the supposed limits, if that's all that your concept calls for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-16-2019, 11:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-16T08:37:57-07:00","id":1312575,"title":"","content":"Yeah, agreed with everything.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I said, the mage might not know about sphere and arete points, but he know about quintessence,tass and individual effects. He also could know how much spells he can put in a wonder before it blows, for example.</div> </div> </div> </div>My example proved the contrary. The 4 effects on the Time Watch are all part of the same &quot;power&quot; - how could <i>my mage</i> know that the watch has four &quot;effects&quot; rather than one?.<br /> <br /> &quot;Effect&quot; it's a game term, a mage has no way in hell to notice if he's casting one or two because effects aren't a discrete entity &quot;in game&quot;. A single <i>act of Focus </i>*can* contain any number of Effects.<br /> <br /> That you can't differentiate between these two dimensions (rules and fiction) it's what makes you unable to understand my point below. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What your point here? I already covered that when talked about how mages doesn't choose what they believe or can do. Also, the complexity is reflected in paradigm and focus. </div> </div> </div>My point, hence, was that what mages know about magick in game and their Focus are one and the same. Focus <i>it's how you believe magick works</i> - this includes what do you think a &quot;discrete effect&quot; or an &quot;amount of Quintessence&quot; is. Focus includes how many effects you think a Wonder has and could have, and how much each effect weights on that wonder, because this is all part of Magick. And Focus determines all of these things for the mage<i> with complete disregard of the rules.</i><br /> <br /> You seem to be trying to imply that your mage should &quot;infer&quot; the rules IOC. That he may call &quot;effects&quot; for another name, but still knows where an effect starts, where it ends, and how much it costs. In Game...<br /> <br /> ...But WoD was created with the intention of rules bending to the narrative and not the other way arround. WoD has, as Dataweaver puts it, a &quot;Concept is King&quot; approach where the rules are more a suggestion than law. Trying to infer the rules IOC to state that mages <i>should </i>be taking advantage on them and it's unrealistic if they don't it's not going to fly. It's against ths spirit of the rules and a clear example of trying to justify powergaming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-16-2019, 11:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-16T11:28:47-07:00","id":1312605,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> I agree with your first post. Focus/paradign IS the defining factor of how the wonder will work, and one of the ones that define if it can be created/used by the mage. <br /> But that us not what Aleph is saying. He simply considered slowing someone else with the hourglas is not compatible with the creator's paradigm, which I didn't even said what it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312575#post1312575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, agreed with everything.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> My example proved the contrary. The 4 effects on the Time Watch are all part of the same &quot;power&quot; - how could <i>my mage</i> know that the watch has four &quot;effects&quot; rather than one?.<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;Effect&quot; it's a game term, a mage has no way in hell to notice if he's casting one or two because effects aren't a discrete entity &quot;in game&quot;. A single <i>act of Focus </i>*can* contain any number of Effects.<br /> <br /> That you can't differentiate between these two dimensions (rules and fiction) it's what makes you unable to understand my point below. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> My point, hence, was that what mages know about magick in game and their Focus are one and the same. Focus <i>it's how you believe magick works</i> - this includes what do you think a &quot;discrete effect&quot; or an &quot;amount of Quintessence&quot; is. Focus includes how many effects you think a Wonder has and could have, and how much each effect weights on that wonder, because this is all part of Magick. And Focus determines all of these things for the mage<i> with complete disregard of the rules.</i><br /> <br /> You seem to be trying to imply that your mage should &quot;infer&quot; the rules IOC. That he may call &quot;effects&quot; for another name, but still knows where an effect starts, where it ends, and how much it costs. In Game...<br /> <br /> ...But WoD was created with the intention of rules bending to the narrative and not the other way arround. WoD has, as Dataweaver puts it, a &quot;Concept is King&quot; approach where the rules are more a suggestion than law. Trying to infer the rules IOC to state that mages <i>should </i>be taking advantage on them and it's unrealistic if they don't it's not going to fly. It's against ths spirit of the rules and a clear example of trying to justify powergaming.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are completely wrong. For a mage to cast an effect he must visualise and put his intent through his awakened Will and use his practice/instument to help him do what he desires. This is repeatedly explained in the books. For example, a martial artist that wants to buff stamina:<br /> He first wishes &quot;I want to make my body resilient&quot;. Then he uses his Will and focus by meditating(instrument) on what he desires and the spell is cast.<br /> If he wants to put more than one effect into a single spell, he also needs to wish both effects separately.<br /> For example, the martial artist that want to become stronger as well. He must think &quot;I wish to make my body more resilient and stronger&quot; and them he does that with the same instruments (meditation, maybe). In-game he is wishing for two different results and if he wants to do that in a single spell, he must use the rules of casting more than 1 effect in off-game rules.<br /> <br /> The spell, in-game, has a begging (the intent), middle(the use of Will and focus) and end (the intent turning into reality, or failing). <br /> <br /> Therefore, the mage knows exactly how much spells he is casting with how much effects. The same goes for wonder creation that is nothing more than casting effects on special objects with some other implications (Quintessence, etc.)<br /> <br /> Also, the use of Quintessence can be controlled and known as well. A witch using X ml of werewolf blood in her ritual, the technomancer using X VAh to power his device. Of course, some instruments might be harder to count, like the meditation and using chi. But the mage that is an expert of the use of this technique can differentiate the ammount of chi he uses in each effect, dragonball style. Easier if he has Prime sphere, which is a requisite to craft wonders.<br /> <br /> So, the mage indeed CAN determine how much effects he can put in his wonder. <br /> <br /> About the wonder, the effects does not have to be similar or in accordance with the wonder, just, as I said, to the paradigm. A mage could create a bracelet imbued with runes and symbols of different greek gods. Ares to throw fire in his enemies, Chronos to stop time, Hermes to a teleport effect, and etc. And when the mage speaks the sacred runes meaning, the respective effect is activated. The details and story of the wonder should be decided as well, but they impose no limitation other than the paradigm.<br /> <br /> My initial point in this thread is that is very rare to not be able to put more effects in a wonder, unless you have serious creativity issues or want to raise this ridiculous &quot;no powergaming&quot; flag by not getting something you paid for in char creation. It's like buying Allies 5, but only getting 3 folks because your char is shy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","user":"Lord Revan","date":". 05-16-2019, 03:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-17T09:22:55-07:00","id":1312774,"title":"","content":"Firts things first, because this was your real issue:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">My initial point in this thread is that is very rare to not be able to put more effects in a wonder, unless you have serious creativity issues or want to raise this ridiculous &quot;no powergaming&quot; flag by not getting something you paid for in char creation. It's like buying Allies 5, but only getting 3 folks because your char is shy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your example fails to recognize the fact that you can buy Allies 3 if you just want to have 3 friends, but you <i>can't </i>buy Wonder 1 if you just want to have a watch that Stops Time. Wonder 5 it's mandatory for the latter while it's not for the former. <br /> <br /> We, (and I said we because pretty much everyone in the thread seems to agree) are regarding your problem as the problem of a min-maxer because you don't want a watch that stops time, you want to have as many powers as you can have with that expenditure of dots. And that's number-crunching. If you tought story-first, then you would FIRST think what your character needs and THEN see how much it's that going to cost, w/o caring so much about what those &quot;points&quot; can give if you tought otherwise. <br /> <br /> If you want a watch that stops time for <i>story </i>reasons then you've just got what you paid for: Wanted a clock, got a clock, what are you complaining for?. Only when you start thinking in an <i>economical </i>fashion, which it's the same train of tought that leads to min-maxing, you can realize that you could have had so much more for the same &quot;price&quot;. This kind of thinking, so common in D&amp;D and wargames, it's what I call powergaming<br /> <br /> When you want something, you can go buy it at the store next door, or you can look at the offers first and see how much you can get for the same money if you optimize your buying...two different ways of buying.<br /> If you want to save one it's clearly superior, but for some people not loosing sleep calculating how much can they save it's a matter of pride.<br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> About the rest: I'm going to quote M20 directly, and then I will explain what you're getting wrong<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You are completely wrong. <b>For a mage to cast an effect he must visualise and put his intent through his awakened Will and use his practice/instument to help him do what he desires</b>. This is repeatedly explained in the books.</div> </div> </div> </div>Let's see what the books have to say about this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> M20 p.528</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As mentioned earlier, <b>every magickal act is referred to<u>,<span style=\"font-size:16px\">in game terms</span></u>, as an Effect.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>You do notice that Effect it's a System related word. Right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.371</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u>The standard method for running Mage magick involves asking a player,</u><b>\u201cWhat\u2019s your character doing, and how is she doing it?\u201d </b>If she responds with something like, \u201cI\u2019m using Forces 3/ Prime 2 to inflict aggravated damage,\u201d ask her, \u201cBut <b><u><span style=\"font-size:14px\">what </span>is YOUR CHARACTER doing</u><i> in order to make that happen</i></b>?\u201d This way,<u> you invite the player to think in terms of the mage and her practices rather than in terms of the game systems involved</u>.<b> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u>Once </u></span>you\u2019ve figured out <span style=\"font-size:12px\">how </span><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">the character</span> </span>is weaving belief, Will, and knowledge</b> <b>into a magickal Effect</b><u>,</u><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><u><span style=\"font-size:14px\">then</span></u> </span>check Chapter Ten for the rules </b>involved. This way, you put story first and let it guide the rules and their results</div> </div> </div> </div>Note how the ST has to ask the WHAT apart from the HOW. More importantly, note that the HOW it's to answer <i>in character</i> about the Effect. Notice how there are two levels here: On one level <i>the mage does something</i> (I'm assuming you don't think that mages need to explain in game what they're doing out loud, they just do it. IOC Level), and in the other level the player explains what he's doing to weave the effect (I'm HOPING you notice how the whole talk about belief, Will and knowledge sits at the same level as Effect, which it's a game term and thus not something that the character will think. OOC Level). <i>Weaving belief, Will and knowledge into an Effect it's what needs to be explained, and thus it can't be the explanation</i><br /> <br /> And if you go to the &quot;examples in play&quot;, you will notice players never talk like you do about visualization and Will, they just describe what the character do in terms of the Focus, and there's a good reas<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.372</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> In Mage 20 <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>focus</b> </span><b>refers to the entire act of focusing Arete through belief, activity, and tools.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Notice how the ST has to ask what the player it's doing, the Focus, and how that reffers to the entire act of Arete+ belief + tools you're mentioning. I think you're confusing Focus and Tools, but Focus can't be treated as a separate element from Will and intention, they're all the same thing, and thus for crying out loud: MAGES DON'T THINK ABOUT WILL AS SOMETHING SEPARATED FROM THEIR FOCUS (like if they were different parts of the spellcasting)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.567</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Belief makes the mage. <b>Belief in something \u2013gods, science, spirits, technology, one\u2019s own place in the universe, <u>perhaps </u>simply the conviction that one\u2019s Will is enough</b> to move the world \u2013 is utterly essential if you want to change reality. Faithless folks cannot use True Magick, for such Arts and Sciences depend upon belief. That belief provides the core of focus: that lens through which a mage does what he does.</div> </div> </div> </div>While it's possible for a mage to believe in the Awakened Will, it would be preposterous for a mage that <i>doesn't </i>believe magick comes from &quot;Awakened Will&quot; to state that he does that. And M20 does state that could be the case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p. 372</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">In the movie Ink \u2013 which, incidentally, is a film every Mage player should see \u2013 the Pathfinder Jacob manipulates a series of events in order to stage a car accident<b>. <span style=\"font-size:14px\">He doesn\u2019t say, \u201cHey, I\u2019m a mage, so I Will that car to crash.</span></b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">\u201d</span> Instead, he literally orchestrates people and elements to collide in a way that fits his focus<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> It\u2019s a sublime moment of movie magic that also perfectly suits the style of freeform magick of Mage: The Ascension. If you want to see what enforcing a focus looks like, this scene</div> </div> </div> </div>Note how grabbing on the Willworking Paradigms to justify that you know these things it's not really how Mage it's supposed to be played according to the author. Of course what the author thinks shouldn't interfer with YOUR roleplay, the point here it's that it's simply wrong to assume Mages are supposed to think in terms of <i>visualizing and willing something to happen</i>. That's an easy way to describe things in general for the perspective of the player that needs his character to do stuff, and thus it's used a lot much like Spheres are used a lot, but it's not what most mages do in their daily lifes<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.568</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Despite some misconceptions suggested by Mage\u2019s first edition, a mage doesn\u2019t use her focus to fool witnesses while picking Reality\u2019s pocket. Focus is an intrinsic element of every mage, and although an individual might eventually recognize that she herself is the true focus of her Arts,<b> few mages ever reach the level where they can depend upon nothing except themselves</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not just a &quot;help&quot;, it's an intrinsic element that remains there <i>even </i>for those mages that have the intelectual conviction of actually being the source of their magick (which it's not necessarily the same as believing you are a Willworker, that's just one example that just happens to be aligned with the terminology the books use)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> For example, a martial artist that wants to buff stamina:<br /> He first wishes &quot;I want to make my body resilient&quot;. Then he uses his Will and focus by meditating(instrument) on what he desires and the spell is cast.</div> </div> </div> </div>Let's see if you can catch the differences:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.580</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Essentially, the artist manifests her will through clarity, energy, and activity. In game terms, she uses that art as a mystic focus.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;In game terms&quot;, what you're describing it's a<i> mystic focus</i>. It's something technomancers don't have. You're sepparating will from focus, the book doesn't do that (<i>here)</i>. <br /> <br /> But even then all that talk about manifesting will it's an extremely simplified version of what's written above in the section sbout MS(note that the word &quot;essentially&quot; get's used, that word implies that the author will tell you the defining aspects of all of the above). If you pay attention, in ALL of the text above &quot;awakened will&quot; it's not mentioned...why would that be?. <br /> <br /> When the books talk about Awakened Will most of the time it's so the player understands what he's doing divorced from each Paradigm, because if the book had to enumerate every explanation about &quot;why&quot; magick works from each Paradigm it would be impossible to talk about mages...but In Game mages use different (more complex) theories to understand power. Otherwise they would all be Willworkers, which it's something that would clash with many magical philosophies mages hold and it's explicitly denied.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>If he wants to put <u>more than one effect</u> into<u> a single spell</u></b>, he also needs to wish both effects separately.</div> </div> </div> </div>1rst:<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.528</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a roleplaying note, <b>please remember that characters NEVER call what they\u2019re doing \u201cEffects.\u201d</b> A witch will cast a spell, a Black Suit will activate a Procedure, a scientist will pull a lever or punch a command into her keyboard...That\u2019s game talk, not character talk.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is not a nitpick, it's important because for once here you're wrong not only in suggesting that MA it's a form of wishcraft, <b><i>but also about the actual rules involved</i></b>: <br /> <br /> While no single spell can contain more than one Effect (System Wise), a single Effect can make two or more discrete things happen. For instance, if I use the Divide successes rule, I can make two fireballs appear in the same turn, I just need one extra success. I grab a wand, I grab another wand and I start fireballing left and right, no Human or Wizard on his right mind would say I'm NOT casting to spells on the same round...<br /> <br /> ...Of course, the <i>system </i>knows it's in truth one spell with two targets. That's why you need to sepparate character knowledge from system knowledge. My character doesn't know how many successes he got, if anything he knows that splitting your power casting two spells in a second it's harder than casting one (and, perhaps, that temporal spells don't make this any easier for some reason) .<br /> <br /> This was better explained in Revised than in M20:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Revised p.155</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A mage can only conjure an Effect per turn...If you want your mage to realize multiple activities at the same time , you would need to conjure an Effect that does several different things simultaneously</div> </div> </div> </div>And we know it's not retconned by M20 because it allows to craft complex Effects that make several things simultaneously:<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> M20 p.562</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dr. Hans von Roth leads cops on a merry chase. Laughing behind the wheel of his reengineered Aston Martin Vanquish (its copper-silver chassis blurred into an eye-watering streak), he shifts into 7th gear, activating the Trans-Temporal Warp Drive\u2026<br /> <br /> The Spheres involved break down like this: <b>Correspondence to</b> skip the car from one place to another through intervening distances in order<b> to avoid hitting other vehicles</b>, <b>Time to accelerate the car</b> beyond its normal capabilities,<b> Forces to handle the gravitational physics involved, and Matter to keep the car from flying apart </b>under stresses it\u2019s not designed to endure. Because Dr. von Roth Chapter Ten: The Book of Magic 563 has been engineering such capabilities into the car, it functions as a unique, personalized instrument; and because those capabilities work only for Dr. Roth, the car is a focus instrument, not a Wonder</div> </div> </div> </div>Also note how in the &quot;In Game&quot; paragraph there's no mention about wishing anything to happen, using Will, or any other word that should be reserved for player talk or very specific Paradigms that probably interpret them different from the players...<br /> <br /> NOTE: To tackle the potential objection of casting two fireballs at one target: Spell cosmetics, fluff wise, it's &quot;free&quot;. You don't pay more for a green fireball, and you don't pay more for a splitted fireball unless that <i>does</i> something. That's how Technocrats can &quot;&quot;&quot;cast&quot;&quot;&quot; machinegun fire. System wise the damage overall would be the same as one fireball and the damage would be caused all at once (albeit a case for causing damage twice on a round if you halve the damage caused on each instance can be made...that would just make your attack easier to resist. But I disgress), but IOC my mage it's still hurling two fireballs. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The spell, in-game, has a begging (the intent), middle(the use of Will and focus) and end (the intent turning into reality, or failing). Therefore, the mage knows exactly how much spells he is casting with how much effects. </div> </div> </div>You're confounding the mage with the player here. I've provided plenty of proof already about mages in general not talking about &quot;intent&quot;, &quot;Will&quot;, &quot;effects&quot; in the sense as the rulebook talks about them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.529</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As detailed in Chapters One, Two, and Six, mages focus their Effects through a combination of paradigm, practice, and<br /> tools. Step Two involves thinking from your mage\u2019s perspective \u2013<b> ignoring the dots on your character sheet in favor of whether or not your character would believe in what you have in mind</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 p.507</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Remember, too, that mages don\u2019t choose their tools based on convenience, but rather upon what they believe they need to do in order to alter reality. A mad scientist toils in her laboratory to create a portable transmutation ray, then carries that ray around with her, aims it, and turns it on before the Effect starts changing things. It would be more convenient, in game terms, for her to simply glare at an enemy and turn him into a newt. Even if she has Life 5 on her character sheet, however, she wouldn\u2019t do things that way because she\u2019s a scientist, not a witch. Sure, she probably accepts that witches have power; that kind of power isn\u2019t her style, though\u2026 and thus, it does not work for her. <br /> Eventually, certain mages learn to BE the focus rather than to NEED the focus. And at that point, they\u2019re able to discard tools, as described earlier in this section. Still, as we explained in the sidebar about the one-inch punch, you can\u2019t ditch your instruments just because you think it\u2019s possible to do so. <b>Magick is an extension of the mage, and tools direct that mage\u2019s beliefs and bring his magick into being.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>You need to take these paragraphs to heart instead of merely reading it as a limitation on Instruments. This is not D&amp;D where these things are placed just to add game balance, this is a <i>philosophy. </i>So, mages don't choose their tools based on convenience nor take in count their dots to cast spells, but make their Wonders based on convenience and the amount of points it costs?. I don't think so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">About the wonder, the effects does not have to be similar or in accordance with the wonder, just, as I said, to the paradigm. A mage could create a bracelet imbued with runes and symbols of different greek gods. Ares to throw fire in his enemies, Chronos to stop time, Hermes to a teleport effect, and etc. And when the mage speaks the sacred runes meaning, the respective effect is activated. The details and story of the wonder should be decided as well, but they impose no limitation other than the paradigm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure he can, but the point it's that you need to explain it <br /> <br /> There's a reason why both I and Dataweaver accused you of min-maxing here, you're thinking on ways to optimize wonders first and Paradigm second. In your own words: &quot;It's a limitation&quot;. But this is the thing, IOC Paradigm it's not a limitation, IOC PAradigm <i>it's all there is</i> <br /> <br /> Hence, the Mage doesn't need to explain why he choosed to make a sub-optimal Wonder to his Mentor, because both Wonders and optimization are part of the OOC world. IOC the Mentor doesn't know that adding as many Effects as the Sphere level it's possible because he doesn't know about Effects or Spheres, he only knows about his Paradigm, and within Paradigm a Wonder would have as many effects as it's Concept requires,<u> no more, no less.</u> <br /> <br /> I...don't think I can be clearer that that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-17-2019, 12:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-17T11:38:19-07:00","id":1312801,"title":"","content":"I would add that \u201cwishcraft\u201d <i>is</i> very much a thing in M20; but it's explicitly <i>not</i> the normal way of doing things. Instead, it's facilitated by the Working Without Focus and Growing Beyond the Tools sections, pp.566\u2013567. Working Without Focus involves a Willpower expenditure and a massive difficulty increase; Growing Beyond the Tools involves raising the mage's Arete, and involves letting go of the Instruments in reverse order of their significance to her: she gets to let go of the ones she uses the least first, and holds on to the one she uses the most until the very last. This is also very much Concept-driven.<br /> <br /> Also, I'm not going to go so far as to \u201caccuse\u201d Lord Revan of power-gaming. He <i>is</i> showing signs of the min-maxer mentality. But first, min-maxing isn't quite the same thing as power-gaming: to me, power-gaming is the worst stereotype of the min-maxer, conjuring images of a megalomaniacal player who seeks to dominate the game any way he can. A min-maxer, on the other hand, is merely someone who wants everything that he paid for. I very much understand the mindset: as I said, it took me a long time to realize the problems with it and to overcome them \u2014 decades, really. I don't like the term \u201caccuse\u201d because that has connotations that the person being accused is willfully doing something bad. That's not the case here. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I'd like to emphasize one of Aleph's points here: a distinction needs to be drawn between paying more than you have to for what you get on the one hand, and getting less than you can for what you pay on the other. It's a subtle but important difference between whether or not you're getting \u201cripped off\u201d (the former) or are merely not exploiting a \u201crounding error\u201d.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-17-2019, 03:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-17T12:52:55-07:00","id":1312811,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312774#post1312774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Firts things first, because this was your real issue:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Your example fails to recognize the fact that you can buy Allies 3 if you just want to have 3 friends, but you <i>can't </i>buy Wonder 1 if you just want to have a watch that Stops Time. Wonder 5 it's mandatory for the latter while it's not for the former. <br /> <br /> We, (and I said we because pretty much everyone in the thread seems to agree) are regarding your problem as the problem of a min-maxer because you don't want a watch that stops time, you want to have as many powers as you can have with that expenditure of dots. And that's number-crunching. If you tought story-first, then you would FIRST think what your character needs and THEN see how much it's that going to cost, w/o caring so much about what those &amp;quot;points&amp;quot; can give if you tought otherwise. <br /> <br /> If you want a watch that stops time for <i>story </i>reasons then you've just got what you paid for: Wanted a clock, got a clock, what are you complaining for?. Only when you start thinking in an <i>economical </i>fashion, which it's the same train of tought that leads to min-maxing, you can realize that you could have had so much more for the same &amp;quot;price&amp;quot;. This kind of thinking, so common in D&amp;D and wargames, it's what I call powergaming<br /> <br /> When you want something, you can go buy it at the store next door, or you can look at the offers first and see how much you can get for the same money if you optimize your buying...two different ways of buying.<br /> If you want to save one it's clearly superior, but for some people not loosing sleep calculating how much can they save it's a matter of pride.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, we disagree on what a powergamer is, and thats ok. I bet a lot of people here will also disagree on this matter. To me, a powergamer is someone that ONLY looks for numbers and results, never worrying about creating a fun character, an interesting story or anything.<br /> D&amp;D is famous for this, because there are people who indeed play it powergaming. If you are an old player you might know the character created in the Wizards of the Coast thread named Pun-pun. It was a nearly omnipotent kobold character that was made following the rules and obviously taking advantage of massive flaws in the rules of tha game. Pun-pun was never meant to be played (he could do literally anything, even reaching godhood), but I know a lot of players try to build characters not as strong as him, but the best they can, even if this means that the character will make no sense at all and not giving a shit to his story or concept.<br /> RPG is still a game, where the players can make choices and build characters. It is natural that a player will want to have the most powerfull character he can, inside that character concept. I bet the majority of players feel excited when playing a character that they think is powerfull and even more when that power grows in time. <br /> Also, Mage has the element of power constantly reminded. Where mortals can do amazing things with their awakened Will. We see that a lot in the books, mages always trying to become more and more powerfull, never settling with what they have.<br /> <br /> So, lets agree that we have different definitions for powergaming, and please, stop pointing fingers or judging people that play a game style slightly different than yours.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312774#post1312774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <hr /><br /> About the rest: I'm going to quote M20 directly, and then I will explain what you're getting wrong<br /> ()<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think you might understood my point wrong in some parts.<br /> <br /> About the distinction of an effect. Yes, mages dont see the action as an effect, they dont call it that, but they see it, as your M20 quote said, as a spell (mystics) or procedure (technos). <br /> None of what you posted or quoted implies that the mage doesnt know how many spells he is casting. In fact, it is weird to think that a mage cannot differentiate when a spell ends and another begins. It\u00b4s his Will that is changing reality afterall.<br /> And dude, everything we do follows this principle (including real life magick that were inspirations for the author, thats why I brought intent into the discussion) of intent-action-result. Even to get out of your bed this morning you first decided to get up (intent), moved your limbs in a way to accomplish your goal (equivalent of focus, the way you make your intent be made in reality), and got up (the result of your intent+actions).<br /> It doesnt matter: in-game, out-game, real-life Every action must follow this script. Again, in-game, the character must want to cast a spell to accomplish something, take some action that will result in what he wants, and then the spell takes effect. In you Jacobs example, he might not say I Will that car to crash, but he MUST Will it to begin using his focus, otherwise the cars wont crash!!<br /> <br /> Another thing: we have 3 realities to analise. First, we have out of game reality, where the player follow the steps you quoted from M20. Second, we have in-game reality, where the character decides to cast a spell and use his focus to do it. Lastly, there is the real truth in-game, that 99,99% of the mages dont know, that is the purple paradigm. That in reality (in-game), spells are nothing more than the mages Will manifesting his desire. That he doesnt need any of his instruments to cast spells. That was what I was talking before, not that mages can Willwork (they can,when reach Arete 10). <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312774#post1312774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're confounding the mage with the player here. I've provided plenty of proof already about mages in general not talking about &amp;quot;intent&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Will&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;effects&amp;quot; in the sense as the rulebook talks about them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, they might not call it effect or whatever, but they can differentiate one spell from another. <br /> Your point was that the creator of the wonder could not know differentiate one spell from several spells and that is just not true.<br /> Just as you, as a human being, can differentiate (and count) the actions you make (casting spells in-game are just this, actions: the act of the character using his focus).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312774#post1312774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You need to take these paragraphs to heart instead of merely reading it as a limitation on Instruments. This is not D&amp;D where these things are placed just to add game balance, this is a <i>philosophy. </i>So, mages don't choose their tools based on convenience nor take in count their dots to cast spells, but make their Wonders based on convenience and the amount of points it costs?. I don't think so.</div> </div> </div> </div>I answered that in my last post already: The mage cannot use convenient instruments because he didnt chose them, the player did (and can choose them conveniently if he wants).<br /> While the mage is made by the player, the wonder is made by the mage (which in turn is made by the player). And mages always want to become more powerfull. Hence, the mage SHOULD make his wonder based on convenience.<br /> And my point since the OP was that mages (in-game) have no reason to not be based on convenience, since they have nothing limiting how much spells he can put into the wonder other than the wonder spell holding capability. And the risk of making it (same as magick<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />aradox) and the cost (skill,time and Quintessence).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312774#post1312774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure he can, but the point it's that you need to explain it <br /> <br /> There's a reason why both I and Dataweaver accused you of min-maxing here, you're thinking on ways to optimize wonders first and Paradigm second. In your own words: &amp;quot;It's a limitation&amp;quot;. But this is the thing, IOC Paradigm it's not a limitation, IOC PAradigm <i>it's all there is</i> <br /> <br /> Hence, the Mage doesn't need to explain why he choosed to make a sub-optimal Wonder to his Mentor, because both Wonders and optimization are part of the OOC world. IOC the Mentor doesn't know that adding as many Effects as the Sphere level it's possible because he doesn't know about Effects or Spheres, he only knows about his Paradigm, and within Paradigm a Wonder would have as many effects as it's Concept requires,<u> no more, no less.</u> <br /> <br /> I...don't think I can be clearer that that.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said earlier, the mage can count how many spells he is casting. He know that an object of this power can hold as much as 5 spells. He made a wonder that only produces one spell. His mentor goes and asks Why the hell didnt you created it to produce X,Y,Z,W spells as well like I asked you? I already provided the means required: I taught you how to cast those spells and I know you can, I gave you free access to Quintessence, I gave you time and this amazing lab to do it, and I know you have the skills to build it. Whats wrong with you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T09:20:28-07:00","id":1314102,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1312801#post1312801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I'm not going to go so far as to \u201caccuse\u201d Lord Revan of power-gaming. He <i>is</i> showing signs of the min-maxer mentality. But first, min-maxing isn't quite the same thing as power-gaming: to me, power-gaming is the worst stereotype of the min-maxer, conjuring images of a megalomaniacal player who seeks to dominate the game any way he can. A min-maxer, on the other hand, is merely someone who wants everything that he paid for. I very much understand the mindset: as I said, it took me a long time to realize the problems with it and to overcome them \u2014 decades, really. I don't like the term \u201caccuse\u201d because that has connotations that the person being accused is willfully doing something bad. That's not the case here.</div> </div> </div> </div>To me it isn't a bad thing. In some gaming communities (in particular in PC comunities) power-gaming isn't seen as any worse than roleplaying. It's just a different way of playing (and they may not be contradictory, I be seen many builds that are both maxed and very flavorful within the circles of self-proclaimed powergamers). I'm aware that's not the case within WoD comunity, but that's because WoD comunity has a very particular mindset where such activities are frowned uppon. Number-crunching it's a sin and how you dare to buy things at 5 in the first place...<br /> <br /> What you call power-gamer it's what I would call a munchkin. But I understand that these may be interchangeable. IN that sense I have to clarify that what I mean was &quot;min-maxer&quot;, and not someone that would be disruptive in the table.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">About the distinction of an effect. Yes, mages dont see the action as an effect, they dont call it that, but they see it, as your M20 quote said, as a spell (mystics) or procedure (technos).<br /> None of what you posted or quoted implies that the mage doesnt know how many spells he is casting. In fact, it is weird to think that a mage cannot differentiate when a spell ends and another begins. It\u00b4s his Will that is changing reality afterall.</div> </div> </div> </div>You don't understand. What I tried to higlight it's that Effect doesn't need to be the same as what your mage calls a Spell, because Effect it's an OOC terminology and what constitutes a spell it's a component of belief.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And dude, everything we do follows this principle (including real life magick that were inspirations for the author, thats why I brought intent into the discussion) of intent-action-result. Even to get out of your bed this morning you first decided to get up (intent), moved your limbs in a way to accomplish your goal (equivalent of focus, the way you make your intent be made in reality), and got up (the result of your intent+actions).<br /> It doesnt matter: in-game, out-game, real-life Every action must follow this script. Again, in-game, the character must want to cast a spell to accomplish something, take some action that will result in what he wants, and then the spell takes effect. In you Jacobs example, he might not say I Will that car to crash, but he MUST Will it to begin using his focus, otherwise the cars wont crash!! </div> </div> </div>Dude, I just don't have the words: You think that everything you Will in life can be achieved with a single action?. If you want to end the University, is that a single Action? or it requires several actions (going to the class, studying, aproving the exams) with the same <b>purpose</b>?. Intending something to hapen doersn't mean that you just need to do one thing<br /> <br /> More importantly, <i>one Effect can accomplish many different things you're willing to happen,</i> and that you could will with sepparate Effects instead if you have the time: The <i>very rules</i> state that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Book of Secrets p.267</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Can a Mage Cast Multiple Effects in a Single Casting?</b><br /> <b>Yes, assuming those Effects are all part of a conjunctional Effect</b>, as described under Conjunctional (Combined) Sphere- Effects, Mage 20, p. 511. Otherwise no \u2013<b> a mage cannot cast more than one Effect within the same turn</b> \u2013 see Mage 20, p. 523, under the heading for Time Contraction or Dilation /\u201cBullet Time\u201d /Rewind Time.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you use the word Effect with the same meaning in game and out of game (like you do, Revan), this paragraph it's self-contradictory. On one hand it's saying yes you can cast more than one Effect on the same turn,and on the other it's saying that you can't cast more than one Effect on the same turn (it just seems like it, but its a single conjunctional Effect). This is illogical unless <i>Effect isn't the same in the question and in the body of the question...</i>and this is ALL I'm talking about here<br /> <br /> If you can cause with one (conjunctional) Effect the same thing that normaly would require two Effects (and two sepparate Spells). Two actions that are divorced of each other, how can you say that you aren't casting two Spells or however you want to call your magickal actions on the same round?<br /> <br /> I gave a clear example where a single Effect it's used to cast two Spells (at least as far as the mage understand it. If a fireball it's a spell, two are two spells). While a mage can't cast more than one Effect per turn, that single Effect could involve any number of &quot;spells&quot; and &quot;incantations&quot; in order to take place before the effect depending on how the Paradigm works, much like an Akashick migth need to do several Katas before the Effect takes place. This is Irrefutable proff:<b> What a mage calls Spells doesn't have to coincide always with what the rules call Effects</b><br /> <br /> <b>The mage knows how many Spells he's casting, he doesn't know how many EFFECTS it's casting </b>. You think it's mere semantics, but it's not just semantics<br /> <br /> Sure, you need to Intent something to happen to cast a Spell, but since Spells *aren't* Effects because Effects are a game terminology to define what the mage it's doing while Spells are what the mage thinks he's doing, you don't know that the Spells you Willed to happen constitute <i>exactly one</i> Effect. You know you caused things to happen, and you know exactly how many things you did to make those things happen, but you don't know that these things follow the OOC rules of Effects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just as you, as a human being, can differentiate (and count) the actions you make (casting spells in-game are just this, actions: the act of the character using his focus).</div> </div> </div> </div>How many actions take to summon a spirit?. Maybe it's just one phrase, maybe it's a full ritual filled of actions, including several incantations, writting pentagrams. Who are you to say all of this is One Enchantment?. And if it isn't one spell but 7, how would you know that wonders can hold 5 spells?<br /> <br /> And at the same time doing one thing doesn't guarantee you will be casing only one Effect because several Effects could be casted using the same ongoing ritual, like the Wonder example I gave<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence, the mage SHOULD make his wonder based on convenience.<br /> <br /> And my point since the OP was that mages (in-game) have no reason to not be based on convenience, since they have nothing limiting how much spells he can put into the wonder other than the wonder spell holding capability. And the risk of making it (same as magick<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />aradox) and the cost (skill,time and Quintessence). <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">As I said earlier, the mage can count how many spells he is casting. He know that an object of this power can hold as much as 5 spells. He made a wonder that only produces one spell. His mentor goes and asks Why the hell didnt you created it to produce X,Y,Z,W spells as well like I asked you? I already provided the means required: I taught you how to cast those spells and I know you can, I gave you free access to Quintessence, I gave you time and this amazing lab to do it, and I know you have the skills to build it. Whats wrong with you?</span></span><br /> <br /> </div> </div> </div>I think this deserves to be higligthed because it's the reason I fell I had to answer so hard on what's just a playing style: You're saying that NOT making a Wonder with as many spells as possible it's bad roleplaying (at least if the mage wants to be as powerful as possible, like many mages do).<br /> <br /> I say it's not because mages do not know about these things IN GAME.<br /> <br /> What do others think about this?. Mages obviously know when they're using a Procedure/Rote and how many (except, maybe, when technocrats fast-cast using their observation as a Focus. I think in this case the Technocrats believe that what happened was going to happen anyway), but do they know that such doings have the rules of Effects (5 can go in a level 5 wonder, can't cast more than one at the same time) or not?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-24-2019, 12:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T15:16:00-07:00","id":1314177,"title":"","content":"It seems that all your arguments revolve around the questioning if a mage knows how many effects he can cast and if he can differentiate them.<br /> <br /> We disagree on the semantics of in and out game of &quot;effect&quot;. In the case of the FAQ contradiction, you could change out-game &quot;effect&quot; to &quot;Arete roll&quot; and in-game effect to &quot;spell&quot;. My points stands.<br /> <br /> You are using the instruments and the action of using them as being considered &quot;effects&quot; as well. <br /> Let's talk about in-game only, since it's where we are having trouble:<br /> Magick, more than anything, requires Will and an intention. The mage fisrt must have an intention in mind. &quot;I want to burn this guy&quot;. Then he will use the methods he knows to turn that into reality. He might use his necklace and visualise his target burning, or whatever. He might also do more than one thing, like visualise holding his necklace and the do a little dance with a moonwalk and finish with a backflip and pointing at the target. Note that he made lots of things, but they are all part of the method he uses to burn people and he knows that. As soon as he finishes all this crap and sees his target burning, he knows his spell ended. Each spell equals an intention and that's how they can count them.<br /> <br /> Now, if the mage wants to get more than one result (out game effect for you) in one single use of his methods,this means he must have this intention in the first place.&quot;I will burn and wind push this guy all at once&quot;. He then proceeds to use his methods for this as well. He knows that he made just one thing because he just used his methods to reach that goal one time.<br /> If he want to burn someone, visualise, dance,backflip and point and the guy is burnt, and after that he wants to wind push the guy and do all that again he knows he has casted 2 spells. Each spell is directly related to the magr intention.<br /> <br /> Your university example is bad, because in Mage, spells tend to be more direct. We don't see effects like &quot;i want to get rich&quot; or &quot;i want to get promoted in my job&quot;. <br /> Also, in Mage getting a degree isn't magick because it isn't directly changing reality. But if we try to fit into magick I would say that your intent is getting a degree, studying is your instrument and getting the degree is the result. One effect.<br /> <br /> Once you understand that a mage CAN differentiate and count his spells, all my other arguments will make sense to you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T17:18:31-07:00","id":1314203,"title":"","content":"Actually, the main point of contention is whether the game should be driven primarily by the rules. It's not that the mages in the game can't sense the rules in the Mage gamebook; it's that they shouldn't <i>have</i> to. To understand <i>our</i> point of view, you should think of the game as being an <i>approximation of</i> what's \u201creally\u201d there, which is why Rule 0 exists. It's why I bend the rules for Wonder Creation and allow six or more Effects in a Wonder is that's what makes sense in setting. <br /> <br /> But more to the point, the converse of this is that the \u201cfive Effects in a Wonder\u201d rule is an approximation \u2014 a \u201crounding error\u201d, if you will: the accounting system in the rules that determines how much a Wonder costs doesn't allow for fractional point accounting, so there's no way to get a discount for having less than five Effects. This is essentially the same kind of \u201crounding error\u201d that exists with how much you can lift: in the rules: Strength is measured in dots. If we were to accept the idea that the rules completely define the setting, that would mean that everyone would be able to carry 20 kg, 50 kg, 100 kg, 200 kg, or 300 kg \u2014 never 45 kg or 55 kg or 63 kg or anything other than those five values. That is, of course, absurd. In a similar way, the \u201cfive Effects in a Wonder\u201d should be viewed as a guideline for what's reasonable, not a hard-and-fast capacity that can always be filled, and must be filled unless you're an idiot. <br /> <br /> And frankly, that very last part is what I object to the most: not even the idea that there's a hard-and-fast capacity of five Effects per Wonder, but that it is wrong <i>not</i> to fill it all the way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T09:46:49-07:00","id":1314311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314203#post1314203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, the main point of contention is whether the game should be driven primarily by the rules. It's not that the mages in the game can't sense the rules in the Mage gamebook; it's that they shouldn't <i>have</i> to. To understand <i>our</i> point of view, you should think of the game as being an <i>approximation of</i> what's \u201creally\u201d there, which is why Rule 0 exists. It's why I bend the rules for Wonder Creation and allow six or more Effects in a Wonder is that's what makes sense in setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>It wouldn't be driven by rules, since i gave in-game reasons.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314203#post1314203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But more to the point, the converse of this is that the \u201cfive Effects in a Wonder\u201d rule is an approximation \u2014 a \u201crounding error\u201d, if you will: the accounting system in the rules that determines how much a Wonder costs doesn't allow for fractional point accounting, so there's no way to get a discount for having less than five Effects. This is essentially the same kind of \u201crounding error\u201d that exists with how much you can lift: in the rules: Strength is measured in dots. If we were to accept the idea that the rules completely define the setting, that would mean that everyone would be able to carry 20 kg, 50 kg, 100 kg, 200 kg, or 300 kg \u2014 never 45 kg or 55 kg or 63 kg or anything other than those five values. That is, of course, absurd. In a similar way, the \u201cfive Effects in a Wonder\u201d should be viewed as a guideline for what's reasonable, not a hard-and-fast capacity that can always be filled, and must be filled unless you're an idiot. </div> </div> </div> </div>Wtf are you talking about? The strength dots descriptions are limits, then if you want to lift 60 kg you must have 3 (limited by 100 IIRC).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314203#post1314203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And frankly, that very last part is what I object to the most: not even the idea that there's a hard-and-fast capacity of five Effects per Wonder, but that it is wrong <i>not</i> to fill it all the way.</div> </div> </div> </div>Never said it was wrong, just that it didn't make sense for power hungries as mages to not make them this way, as we see in the books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T09:59:05-07:00","id":1314313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Wtf are you talking about? The strength dots descriptions are limits, then if you want to lift 60 kg you must have 3 (limited by 100 IIRC). </div> </div> </div>Of course they\u2019re limits. But they\u2019re the same limits for everyone. If you take the rules literally, then anyone who can lift 60 kg can also lift 100 kg; there\u2019s no way in the rules to represent someone whose <i>limit</i> is 60 kg.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T15:01:00-07:00","id":1314379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314313#post1314313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course they\u2019re limits. But they\u2019re the same limits for everyone. If you take the rules literally, then anyone who can lift 60 kg can also lift 100 kg; there\u2019s no way in the rules to represent someone whose <i>limit</i> is 60 kg.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see. That proves my point. Do you buy str 3 and when your char needs to lift 90kg to save himself you just go &quot;nah, my char is in the middle ground and can only lift 80 kg.&quot; <br /> Except that this example doesn't have in-game reasons, like the creation of wonders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T15:13:53-07:00","id":1314381,"title":"","content":"\u2026and you've completely missed <i>my</i> point. But thanks for illustrating the min-max mindset so clearly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T16:35:43-07:00","id":1314399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314381#post1314381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2026and you've completely missed <i>my</i> point. But thanks for illustrating the min-max mindset so clearly.</div> </div> </div> </div>Come on, man, don't make me laugh. This is still a game, and I don't know how you play it, but the players are expected to try to be the best possible. Cut the &quot;roleplay only&quot; crap, otherwise you wouldn't even need a sheet. Tell me this, do your str 3 char only lift 80kg?<br /> <br /> And still, the in-game reasons for wonders remains.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T16:56:11-07:00","id":1314402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314399#post1314399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Come on, man, don't make me laugh. This is still a game, and I don't know how you play it,</div> </div> </div> </div> You've made that abundantly clear. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1295469-ask-the-devs-v2?p=1314339#post1314339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but the players are expected to try to be the best possible.</div> </div> </div> </div> And that's the heart of the min/max mindset. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1295469-ask-the-devs-v2?p=1314339#post1314339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cut the \u201croleplay only\u201d crap, otherwise you wouldn't even need a sheet.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not \u201croleplay only\u201d; it's \u201croleplay first\u201d; and it's not crap. And for the record, I <i>have</i> run games without character sheets; but that's not what this is about. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1295469-ask-the-devs-v2?p=1314339#post1314339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Tell me this, do your str 3 char only lift 80kg?</div> </div> </div> </div> I'll admit that it's never come up, because \u201chow much can you lift?\u201d has never really been all that important to anyone. But I mentioned it to illustrate the difference in our mindsets: if I <i>were</i> to find it important that my character be able to lift no more than 80kg, then I'd give her three dots of Strength and inform the Storyteller that she can only lift 80kg. <br /> <br /> The issue here, as I see it, isn't that I consider min-maxing to be wrong; it's that <i>you</i> consider <i>not</i> min-maxing to be ridiculous. You're flat-out taking it as a given that everybody does it, and responding with incredulity whenever any of us says otherwise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T20:35:20-07:00","id":1314449,"title":"","content":"Fair enough. If you calll the game as its expected to be played min/max i don't care.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314402#post1314402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The issue here, as I see it, isn't that I consider min-maxing to be wrong.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't look like you don't consider. And for sure the others do.<br /> <br /> Also, you forgot the last part:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And still, the in-game reasons for wonders remains. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T20:46:41-07:00","id":1314453,"title":"","content":"I didn't forget it. I consider it to be of secondary importance: it's not something we can resolve until we're on the same page on the more fundamental issue. And frankly, that's not going to happen as long as you're unwilling to consider that there are other perfectly valid approaches to roleplaying besides yours.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T07:24:22-07:00","id":1314701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314449#post1314449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fair enough. If you calll the game as its expected to be played min/max i don't care.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find <i>fascinating </i>that you think that &quot;the players are expected to try to be the best possible&quot;, like, for instance, buying five effects for your five dots wonder &quot;it's how the game it's supposed to be played&quot; in spite of the fact that nearly all canon wonders have only one effect regardless of their cost...<br /> <br /> ...so, in your opinion the authors of the game don't know how the game it's supposed to be played or don't know the rules they've written.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Magick, more than anything, requires Will and an intention. The mage fisrt must have an intention in mind. &quot;I want to burn this guy&quot;. Then he will use the methods he knows to turn that into reality. He might use his necklace and visualise his target burning, or whatever. He might also do more than one thing, like visualise holding his necklace and the do a little dance with a moonwalk and finish with a backflip and pointing at the target. Note that he made lots of things, but they are all part of the method he uses to burn people and he knows that. As soon as he finishes all this crap and sees his target burning, he knows his spell ended. Each spell equals an intention and that's how they can count them. </div> </div> </div>So you count the amount of &quot;intentions&quot; you pull into your Wonder?. That is SO wierd. I would want to see the opinion of a psychologist about counting exactly how many &quot;intentions&quot; you had while acomplishing different activities. I'm pretty sure different people would arrive at very different conclusions when asked to count these.<br /> <br /> I really don't think Intentions aren't something that can be maped so easily to Effects. I can achieve something I intent to do with several Effects (like in most battles) or with only one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, if the mage wants to get more than one result (out game effect for you) in one single use of his methods,this means he must have this intention in the first place.&quot;I will burn and wind push this guy all at once&quot;. He then proceeds to use his methods for this as well. He knows that he made just one thing because he just used his methods to reach that goal one time.<br /> If he want to burn someone, visualise, dance,backflip and point and the guy is burnt, and after that he wants to wind push the guy and do all that again he knows he has casted 2 spells. Each spell is directly related to the magr intention. </div> </div> </div>So, let me get this straight: &quot;I will burn <b>AND </b>wind push this guy all at once&quot; it's just &quot;one goal&quot;...<i>but doing exactly the same thing</i> in a sligthly longer interval (and note that *turn* it's a variable interval in WoD...so <i>it doesn't need to be &quot;all at once&quot; to be in the same turn</i>. One of these spells can be casted one or two seconds after the other and it could still be part of the same turn) it's &quot;two goals&quot;.<br /> <br /> Really?<br /> <br /> If I intent to cast two spells (as defined by me) in a short interval I can map it to &quot;one Effect&quot; but if I intent to do exactly the same thing (casting two spells) with exactly the same goal in mind (burn and hurl/burn then hurl - turn wise both are doable in a single turn) <i>sligthly slower </i>(seconds of difference)<i>, then </i>it can be maped to to two intentions and thus two Effect.<br /> <br /> No, I don't agree that effects can be maped to intentions or goals. Sometimes it can, but not all the time<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your university example is bad, because in Mage, spells tend to be more direct. We don't see effects like &quot;i want to get rich&quot; or &quot;i want to get promoted in my job&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Primal Utility 4: RAW it allows to increase the Resources Background. So yes, it can be an Effect. It just that it can be ALSO be achieved by casting <i>several Effects</i> instead of only one. The whole point of effects not being mapeable to intentions so easily it's that one discrete goal can be achievd either way<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, in Mage getting a degree isn't magick because it isn't directly changing reality. But if we try to fit into magick I would say that your intent is getting a degree, studying is your instrument and getting the degree is the result. One effect. </div> </div> </div>I also need to be inscribed in the institution...that has nothing to do with studying. So, first bureaucracy and then studying?, two effects?<br /> <br /> But a mage COULD be able to do this with just one Effect -or not- depends on their Paradigm and cleverness.<br /> <br /> I mantain that &quot;Intent&quot; it's not the same as what the rules call an &quot;Effect&quot; NOR the same as &quot;a spell&quot;. I can cast several Effects to do one thing I intend to do, like to get rich or to win a fight - or I could do it with just one Effect. Intentions aren't so easily divided and counted as to map then directly to Effects.<br /> <br /> But, more to the point, Mages think in terms of what they Believe they're able to do, even for Willworkers. In that sense Spell it's closer to the concept of Effect (but not necessarily the same, as I've argued before) than &quot;intent&quot;. The science of magick has it's own rules...<br /> <br /> ...And, as Dataweaver pointed out, in a roleplaying-first environment, these rules may prove to be the correct ones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, the main point of contention is whether the game should be driven primarily by the rules. It's not that the mages in the game can't sense the rules in the Mage gamebook; it's that they shouldn't <i>have</i> to. To understand <i>our</i> point of view, you should think of the game as being an <i>approximation of</i> what's \u201creally\u201d there</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed.<br /> <br /> Otherwise mages would have to be to be fools or very lazy in order to not have discovered the &quot;real reality&quot; yet. By maping ranks to effects, timing Rotes/Procedures and calculating the success chance between hundreds of different practicioners, at least organized and scientifically minded groups like the Technocracy should have a pretty solid grasp of the whole Sphere System after so many centuries (it's not THAT complex).<br /> <br /> I just trust that if a power hungry mage only places a single effect on a high level wonder it's because that's the maximum he was capable within his current theory about how magick works. Perhaps with more potent mateirals or a more advanced design he would have been able to place other Effects, but under his knowledge that's what power looks like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> The strength dots descriptions are limits, then if you want to lift 60 kg you must have 3 (limited by 100 IIRC).</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, it's a soft limit. It's the maximum that you can lift comfortably. There's a roll you can make to lift more (and I would argue that Atletism should allow to lift more too in certain environements). Also, if my players wanted to lift 101 kg, I wouldn't make them to roll nor say you need an extra dot...<br /> <br /> The Strength example it's not appropiate because if you want &quot;the maximum you have ever lifted&quot; to be 80kg...you can get Strenght 2 and say that you used Willpower and made it as a Feat of Strenght. That's how most people calculate their maximums anyway, nobody thinks in therm of perfectly precise hard limits to what is &quot;relatively comfortable&quot; and what needs &quot;technique&quot; or &quot;effort&quot; . So I can't see a roleplayer limiting their Strenght that way<br /> <br /> But the &quot;core&quot; point of that whole thread it's that, yes, many groups of players don't care so much on overall efficiency, to the point of nerfing themselves sometimes. And the game was designed with such a mentality, hence why the canon wonders don't have as many Effects as they could (and they could be considered &quot;a trap&quot; for a group that concerns itself with maximum efficiency)<br /> <br /> In game it's justified, canonically, by mages concentrating on their beliefs and not knowing the rules. This is much easier to buy if the OOC rules don't map 1:1 with what IOC it's understood as &quot;magick&quot;, and I <i>really </i>don't think they were intended to","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-27-2019, 10:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T11:32:25-07:00","id":1314743,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I find <i>fascinating </i>that you think that &amp;quot;the players are expected to try to be the best possible&amp;quot;, like, for instance, buying five effects for your five dots wonder &amp;quot;it's how the game it's supposed to be played&amp;quot; in spite of the fact that nearly all canon wonders have only one effect regardless of their cost...<br /> <br /> ...so, in your opinion the authors of the game don't know how the game it's supposed to be played or don't know the rules they've written.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tell me, when you play a game you don't try to be/get the best possible, while respecting the rules and still having fun? The authors simply didn't care about that. They often let pass brutal errors of rules, you think they will be aware of things like this?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you count the amount of &amp;quot;intentions&amp;quot; you pull into your Wonder?. That is SO wierd. I would want to see the opinion of a psychologist about counting exactly how many &amp;quot;intentions&amp;quot; you had while acomplishing different activities. I'm pretty sure different people would arrive at very different conclusions when asked to count these.<br /> <br /> I really don't think Intentions aren't something that can be maped so easily to Effects. I can achieve something I intent to do with several Effects (like in most battles) or with only one.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, let me get this straight: &amp;quot;I will burn <b>AND </b>wind push this guy all at once&amp;quot; it's just &amp;quot;one goal&amp;quot;...<i>but doing exactly the same thing</i> in a sligthly longer interval (and note that *turn* it's a variable interval in WoD...so <i>it doesn't need to be &amp;quot;all at once&amp;quot; to be in the same turn</i>. One of these spells can be casted one or two seconds after the other and it could still be part of the same turn) it's &amp;quot;two goals&amp;quot;.<br /> <br /> Really?<br /> <br /> If I intent to cast two spells (as defined by me) in a short interval I can map it to &amp;quot;one Effect&amp;quot; but if I intent to do exactly the same thing (casting two spells) with exactly the same goal in mind (burn and hurl/burn then hurl - turn wise both are doable in a single turn) <i>sligthly slower </i>(seconds of difference)<i>, then </i>it can be maped to to two intentions and thus two Effect.<br /> <br /> No, I don't agree that effects can be maped to intentions or goals. Sometimes it can, but not all the time</div> </div> </div> </div>Not weird at all and it's quite simple, especially when we are talking about magick, that manifest instantly in the world.<br /> Just understand this: If your intention manifested BEFORE you began another intention, they are separate effects. If both intentions manifested at the same time, by using your instruments once with that couple of intentions in mind, then it's only one effect. In my burn/wind example:<br /> <br /> A) &quot;want to burn&quot;-&gt;instrument-&gt;target burned. &quot;want to push with wind&quot; -&gt;instrument-&gt; target pushed<br /> <br /> B)&quot;Want to burn a wind push&quot;-&gt;instruments-&gt;target burned and wind pushed.<br /> <br /> Option A are 2 separate effects. Option B is a bigger and harder effect. In fact, the msge also knows it is bigger and harder, even thst je doesn't knows how much &quot;successes&quot; are needes. It's quite simple.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Primal Utility 4: RAW it allows to increase the Resources Background. So yes, it can be an Effect. It just that it can be ALSO be achieved by casting <i>several Effects</i> instead of only one. The whole point of effects not being mapeable to intentions so easily it's that one discrete goal can be achievd either way<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I also need to be inscribed in the institution...that has nothing to do with studying. So, first bureaucracy and then studying?, two effects?<br /> <br /> But a mage COULD be able to do this with just one Effect -or not- depends on their Paradigm and cleverness.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you post some reference? I don't know what you talking about.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Otherwise mages would have to be to be fools or very lazy in order to not have discovered the &amp;quot;real reality&amp;quot; yet. By maping ranks to effects, timing Rotes/Procedures and calculating the success chance between hundreds of different practicioners, at least organized and scientifically minded groups like the Technocracy should have a pretty solid grasp of the whole Sphere System after so many centuries (it's not THAT complex).<br /> <br /> I just trust that if a power hungry mage only places a single effect on a high level wonder it's because that's the maximum he was capable within his current theory about how magick works. Perhaps with more potent mateirals or a more advanced design he would have been able to place other Effects, but under his knowledge that's what power looks like.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, the &quot;real reality&quot; is quite simple and for times I wondered why mages didn't figured it out completely, even asked here in the forums.<br /> The thing is, while it is simple, the mages strong beliefs don't let them see it. The &quot;real reality&quot; is that every magick is just the use of the caster enlightened Will. But mages don't know (or don't believe) that, until they can do that themselves, by dropping their instruments conpletely, and that's quite difficult. <br /> <br /> Some aspects of the &quot;real reality&quot; were already studied and discovered, like the spheres and the influence that sleepers have in casting effects.<br /> <br /> The only reason in-game that I can think for a Master not put 5 effects in a 5 dot wonder is the risk of paradox, which he already have everytime he cast an effect, and the lack of resources (tass, etc). But if you are a master willing to build a lvl 5 wonder, this shouldn't be a problem to you. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, it's a soft limit. It's the maximum that you can lift comfortably. There's a roll you can make to lift more (and I would argue that Atletism should allow to lift more too in certain environements). Also, if my players wanted to lift 101 kg, I wouldn't make them to roll nor say you need an extra dot...<br /> <br /> The Strength example it's not appropiate because if you want &amp;quot;the maximum you have ever lifted&amp;quot; to be 80kg...you can get Strenght 2 and say that you used Willpower and made it as a Feat of Strenght. That's how most people calculate their maximums anyway, nobody thinks in therm of perfectly precise hard limits to what is &amp;quot;relatively comfortable&amp;quot; and what needs &amp;quot;technique&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;effort&amp;quot; . So I can't see a roleplayer limiting their Strenght that way</div> </div> </div> </div>The strength trait represent how much you can lift without shitting your pants, without rolls. When you have a str 2 character and you want to lift 80kg, do you say that you can only lift 60 and roll, or you admit that you have the maximum that you bought for?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But the &amp;quot;core&amp;quot; point of that whole thread it's that, yes, many groups of players don't care so much on overall efficiency, to the point of nerfing themselves sometimes. And the game was designed with such a mentality, hence why the canon wonders don't have as many Effects as they could (and they could be considered &amp;quot;a trap&amp;quot; for a group that concerns itself with maximum efficiency)</div> </div> </div> </div> Fair enough. At least there is nothing wrong in have lvl 3 wonders with 3 powers, or lvl 5 wonders with 5 powers. It's a player choice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314701#post1314701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In game it's justified, canonically, by mages concentrating on their beliefs and not knowing the rules. This is much easier to buy if the OOC rules don't map 1:1 with what IOC it's understood as &amp;quot;magick&amp;quot;, and I <i>really </i>don't think they were intended to</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah.. Even that they couldn't count effects (which I showed they can), they could at least feel if the wonder can hold more power. Even if that requires a Prime 1 roll. &quot;I already made it shoot fireballs, lemme see if it can still hold casting strong winds&quot; *proceeds to use Prime*","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T08:28:02-07:00","id":1314950,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Tell me, when you play a game you don't try to be/get the best possible, while respecting the rules and still having fun?</div> </div> </div> </div>It depends on the kind of game and the style of the table. I try not to be a fundamentalist here because I want my characters to be, at least, &quot;playable&quot; with the less amount of fuss, but in general I would have to say that's not my &quot;native&quot; style, no.<br /> <br /> \u200b <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZiqRKlpsavA\" data-code=\"ZiqRKlpsavA\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/ZiqRKlpsavA/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> I started roleplaying with WoD, as did the people that I play with. Now, there are plenty of &quot;min maxers&quot; in WoD (and I've dabbled on it too), but the whole culture behind the game and the fanbase was heavily oriented against it from the very beginning, and that attitude tends to stick. I'm also accustomed to STs houseruling the shit out of every game and placing less value in numbers...<br /> <br /> ...nedless to say, that <i>experience </i>made my first dive into D20 Future quite...memorable...I wasn't aware that there were whole classes you weren't supposed to choose on the grounds of how mechanically <i>useless </i>they are.<br /> <br /> That whole D20 series of games it's a minefield for people that don't do the math. The character I made ended being <i>terrible </i>for what he was supposed to do, and good for very different things. I ended up with a character that was <i>nothing </i>like what I had in mind and nothing like what the fluff of the classes, skills and feats suggested (and I'm not the only WoD player that went to D&amp;D 3.x and had a similar experience).<br /> <br /> Talking in terms of &quot;game theory&quot;: The dominant strategy varies between games. And it's not always to follow the written rules, not when there are <i>unwritten </i>rules that can be as prevalent or more. In a &quot;rules first&quot; traditional game you really need to pay atention to the fine print in order to play your concept, if you don't you end having a character that feels like he can't do anything. In a &quot;roleplaying first&quot; style having a cool character outweights the advantages of min maxing because the ST has every tool to make cool characters feel cool...and to force min-maxers down to earth (to put it ligthly)...Because tabletop RPG it's a social activity first and foremost, you need to get the social cues of the group in order to be the best you can be...and in some WoD old circles to min-max it's never necessary, and in fact can be a very bad idea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The authors simply didn't care about that. </div> </div> </div>WoD authors in general and Mage in particular don't place so much value in the rules, no. Do I think they should place <i>more </i>value in the rules?, Yes becuase that's at least half of the product they sell - placing the Golden Rule first and foremost shouldn't be an excuse to build poor quality rules. That's why I always complain when I see glaring mistakes. A lot of modern RPG - even D&amp;D - have embraced the Golden Rule, and they do have helpful and mostly consistent rules nevertheless<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They often let pass brutal errors of rules, you think they will be aware of things like this? </div> </div> </div>I don't think they're <i>always </i>aware, but I do think they don't sweat it a lot when they <i>do </i>become aware because they have this philosophy of being relaxed with the rules.<br /> <br /> That being said, placing one effect in a 5 dots Wonder it's not an <i>error </i>(the rules <i>do</i> allow it). It <i>could</i> be a mismanagement but in that case the same could be said of choosing cumbersome Instruments, and the game explicitly states that mages do so out of pride, and encourages players to do it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not weird at all and it's quite simple, especially when we are talking about magick, that manifest instantly in the world.<br /> Just understand this: If your intention manifested BEFORE you began another intention, they are separate effects. If both intentions manifested at the same time, by using your instruments once with that couple of intentions in mind, then it's only one effect. In my burn/wind example:<br /> <br /> A) &quot;want to burn&quot;-&gt;instrument-&gt;target burned. &quot;want to push with wind&quot; -&gt;instrument-&gt; target pushed<br /> <br /> B)&quot;Want to burn a wind push&quot;-&gt;instruments-&gt;target burned and wind pushed. </div> </div> </div>You could have your wind manifesting one second after the burn. Your intention A manifested BEFORE that your intention B, but if you didn't wanted to wait the 3 seconds lapse you need to manifest them as one Effect RAW. Since this is a cosmetic effect, it doesn't cost any more.<br /> <br /> Also, people do not normaly cronometer and clasify their intentions and their success with such a precise distinction, as far as any Mage knows what happened first happened first because the providence wanted it to happen first (the mage prayed for both things to happen, but the player chosed to wait one turn for more effect).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Could you post some reference? I don't know what you talking about. </div> </div> </div>For Primal Utility 4 you have Syndicate Revised (the writeup of the Sphere). For achieving it with several effects you only need to think any 4 parts plan to get rich and enact each one with a different Effect<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, the &quot;real reality&quot; is quite simple and for times I wondered why mages didn't figured it out completely, even asked here in the forums. </div> </div> </div>Is it, though? You will find <i>many </i>veterans here that don't believe Spheres are any more &quot;real&quot; than Paradigms, and could point to DA: Mage or to Variant Spheres to prove that they're just a reflection of Belief.<br /> <br /> Now, the fact of the matter it's that DA: Mage explicitly mentions the Spheres, and specially Arete, as a product of the Grand Convocation and the change of mentality mages had between the middle ages and the modern era, rather than a discovery. <i>What you call the &quot;real reality&quot; of magick was canonically stated to be a product of Belief.</i><br /> <br /> But, of course, this is Mage, and thus if you want Spheres to be the real reality beyond Belief (and perhaps even beyond the Consensus) there's also plenty of evidence pointing there...Mage it's self-contradictory like that. Hence, in Mage &quot;the real reality&quot; depends <i>quite a lot</i> on the ST. And this is seen as a feature by many players. After all, in WoD games the Rule Zero it's supposed to be king<br /> <br /> That's why I think it's very healthy to differentiate between the Sphere System and the Spheres IOC...otherwise you will unavoidably clash on contradictions. If I separate these realms I don't have any problem with representing *old* (as DA old) characters using the Spheres to make things easier nor about using Pillars with modern mages. It's just a system, in game the Spheres can still exist as a sort of &quot;common&quot; even if ALL of reality it's based on belief and mages use Pillars to cast their spells.<br /> <br /> There's not 3 layers of Reality. There's only the rules and the fiction. Even if you decide that the fiction has objective truths these are still part of the fiction, and don't need to reflect the rules 1:1 to be <i>allowed </i>by them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some aspects of the &quot;real reality&quot; were already studied and discovered, like the spheres and the influence that sleepers have in casting effects. </div> </div> </div>And yet it's explicitly stated that nobody (and I mean <i>nobody</i>) names the Spheres as part of their actual magick. Do I need to point the part where the Authors talk about how mages <i>never </i>cast spells refering to Spheres. Do I need to argue that Spheres are just a common language and nobod really believes that's how the world works, and the part where it's stated that mages need to Beleive in what they're doing?<br /> <br /> The influence Sleepers have it's a better example, but note that Mages only have a very vague understanding. You may have noticed that mages place an inordinate amount of fear in witnesses, yet<i> never relate Paradox with botches...</i>and yet those are a very important aspect of Paradox, more even than witnesses.<br /> They know that &quot;magick it's harder in front of unbelievers&quot; and that Paradox has something to do with the Consensus...from that to knowing, say, what's the maximum Paradox a certain Effect can cause or <i>exactly </i>how many dots you have in your wheel (and based on that what are the chances of having a backlash) there's a world of difference. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The thing is, while it is simple, the mages strong beliefs don't let them see it. The &quot;real reality&quot; is that every magick is just the use of the caster enlightened Will. But mages don't know (or don't believe) that, until they can do that themselves, by dropping their instruments conpletely, and that's quite difficult. </div> </div> </div>If mages don't know or believe about something so simple as Sphere levels, how could they know about Wonder levels that are so tied to them?, why is Effects a simpler concept to grasp than Spheres?. Spheres at least exist as an Esperanto, and they kinda reflect on the ranks of the Traditions...yet no Tradition believes in them enough as to use them as an actual working Paradigm. Why would they believe in a concept so much more elusive as &quot;Effects&quot; where you need to start &quot;counting intentions&quot; among other things that have nothing to do with the beleifs of mages?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The strength trait represent how much you can lift without shitting your pants, without rolls. When you have a str 2 character and you want to lift 80kg, do you say that you can only lift 60 and roll, or you admit that you have the maximum that you bought for?</div> </div> </div> </div>If I have a Str 2 character my limit it's 50kg and it's a soft limit for a <i>reason</i>. How much you can lift it's something that changes depending on the current mental and physical state, I just don't imagine a roleplayer capping at such a precise hard limit, it just doesn't make any sense.<br /> <br /> Now perhaps I would make a character that it's a lazzy pants, and thus I would simply not roll if he would require to lift more than 60 (60 being just as much effort as he's willing to make - albeit again, it's an oddly precise limit)...but perhaps the situation changes and he finds the balls to try and lift 80th, and it's going to be much more dramatic that way because it means he managed to pierce his limits both physical and mental because *he cares* (like when a mom lifts a car to save her baby)...instead of being just *what he paid for*<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Fair enough. At least there is nothing wrong in have lvl 3 wonders with 3 powers, or lvl 5 wonders with 5 powers. It's a player choice. </div> </div> </div>Of course, there's nothing wrong with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah.. Even that they couldn't count effects (which I showed they can), they could at least feel if the wonder can hold more power. Even if that requires a Prime 1 roll. &quot;I already made it shoot fireballs, lemme see if it can still hold casting strong winds&quot; *proceeds to use Prime* </div> </div> </div>Prime 1 can gauge the power and energy a Wonder has, but not the amount of Effects.<br /> <br /> Prime detects Quintessence, and uses of magick. Wonders have a cost based on their level and the amount of &quot;special properties&quot; (Arete and the optional improvements). A Wonder with a level 5 Effect cost 15 Q, the same as one with a level 3 Effect and Arete 5. The number of Effects <i>per se</i> isn't mentioned as altering the Q cost as far as I can see. Arete rolls DO alter the cost (and define the overal &quot;power&quot; of the Wonder), but because successes are variable (stronger Effects usually require more than weaker ones) you can't know how many Arete rolls and Q went for an certain amount of Effects w/o some serious knowledge of the rules.<br /> <br /> Hence you can't know how much &quot;power&quot; each singular Effect represents inside a Wonder unless you can perfectly issolate each Effect (which, as I've explained, you can't) and thus Prime 1 wouldn't necessarily reveal if the Wonder has more &quot;space&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-28-2019, 12:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T10:31:12-07:00","id":1314967,"title":"","content":"While they're not in the M20 line, there <i>are</i> published modern-day mages who use Foundation and Pillars instead of Arete and Spheres, in the V20 Black Hand book. The Storyteller's free to do what he wants with them, up to and including ignoring then if he doesn't like the notion of mages not using Arete and Spheres; but conversely, if he doesn't mind Foundation and Pillars and for some reason needs something to point to to say that it's OK to do it, he does have that precedent. <br /> <br /> And thanks for adopting the \u201crules first\u201d vs. \u201croleplay first\u201d terminology. It highlights an important point: we're not talking about diametrically opposed play styles, with no middle ground. Rules first people still roleplay, and roleplay first people still pay attention to the roles; the difference it in what they do when there's a conflict between the two.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T10:51:52-07:00","id":1314971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It depends on the kind of game and the style of the table. (...) can be a very bad idea.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was the opposite, I began rpg with AD&amp;D and those little books of fantastic adventures (love them, specially when you get to play a Wizard), was very young at the time and WotC soon released D&amp;D 3.0.<br /> At the same time I began playing WoD, sometimes switching between the 2 systems.<br /> I admit that I have someo of the D&amp;D min/max mentality, but not a unhealthy one. By unhealthy, I mean buy points or getting stuff that would make no sense for you char to have, just to make combos or get advantage in something. That's not the case of the Wonder powers. There is no combo, and depending on the powers, they can fit nicely into a concept. That's why I don't consider what I'm asking min/maxing.<br /> Nice to see your game history. Can't see the video now, but I'll do it later.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">WoD authors in general and Mage in particular don't place so much value in the rules, no. Do I think they should place <i>more </i>value in the rules?, Yes becuase that's at least half of the product they sell - placing the Golden Rule first and foremost shouldn't be an excuse to build poor quality rules. That's why I always complain when I see glaring mistakes. A lot of modern RPG - even D&amp;D - have embraced the Golden Rule, and they do have helpful and mostly consistent rules nevertheless<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I don't think they're <i>always </i>aware, but I do think they don't sweat it a lot when they <i>do </i>become aware because they have this philosophy of being relaxed with the rules.<br /> <br /> That being said, placing one effect in a 5 dots Wonder it's not an <i>error </i>(the rules <i>do</i> allow it). It <i>could</i> be a mismanagement but in that case the same could be said of choosing cumbersome Instruments, and the game explicitly states that mages do so out of pride, and encourages players to do it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, the lack of consistency also piss me off. Other systems are way more careful with this.<br /> I believe people that take cumbersome Instruments always have a way of casting simpler and faster, be by getting other instruments as well or even the rule presented in the Order of Hermes book, to prepare spells. You see, even the authors this time had to help balance the game.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could have your wind manifesting one second after the burn. Your intention A manifested BEFORE that your intention B, but if you didn't wanted to wait the 3 seconds lapse you need to manifest them as one Effect RAW. Since this is a cosmetic effect, it doesn't cost any more.<br /> <br /> Also, people do not normaly cronometer and clasify their intentions and their success with such a precise distinction, as far as any Mage knows what happened first happened first because the providence wanted it to happen first (the mage prayed for both things to happen, but the player chosed to wait one turn for more effect).</div> </div> </div> </div>Didn't get your point, A and B are not intentions, they are two ways of getting the result, being A two separate spells and B just one. It is not about the time spent to the intention come true, but the number of times you had to do something (instrument) before any of you intentions came true. Also, in the B scenario, the mage knows that he has to spend more time and effort(more successes) to make then come true, because he is &quot;wishing&quot; for 2 things at once. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For Primal Utility 4 you have Syndicate Revised (the writeup of the Sphere). For achieving it with several effects you only need to think any 4 parts plan to get rich and enact each one with a different Effect</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, I'm not very familiar with that, but Mage spells don't work as genies wishes, you have to be quite direct in what you want. And surely &quot;want to get rich&quot; doesn't work this way. You'd have to be specific of how you want it, so it would be a sequence of spells.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it, though? <br /> (...) there's a world of difference.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Spheres exist in-game as classifications, created by the hermetics. I know they are not used as paradigms, but there are paradigms that can use this classification. The purple paradigm also is a thing in-game, but few believe in it (mostly the ones that dropped instruments). Paradox and Quintessence are a thing in-game and, while it is not stated that it can be precisely measured (maybe the Etherites did it), it sure can be compared. Mages knows that a sleeper have X ammount of Q and werewolves have 2X.<br /> Also, spells and rore are a thing in-game, and they happen to follow the same intention aspect of out-game rules, because they were based of real world &quot;magick&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If mages don't know or believe about something so simple as Sphere levels, how could they know about Wonder levels that are so tied to them?, why is Effects a simpler concept to grasp than Spheres?. Spheres at least exist as an Esperanto, and they kinda reflect on the ranks of the Traditions...yet no Tradition believes in them enough as to use them as an actual working Paradigm. Why would they believe in a concept so much more elusive as &amp;quot;Effects&amp;quot; where you need to start &amp;quot;counting intentions&amp;quot; among other things that have nothing to do with the beleifs of mages?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because that's how they cast spells. A Chorister that prays for his god to burn someone and after the target is burned to wind push knows he had 2 separate outcomes. While if he prays for his target to be burned and pushed (1 pray) he can count it as one outcome.<br /> <br /> Dude, it's quite simple, I don't know how you didn't get it with my A and B scenarios. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I have a Str 2 character my limit it's 50kg and it's a soft limit for a <i>reason</i>. How much you can lift it's something that changes depending on the current mental and physical state, I just don't imagine a roleplayer capping at such a precise hard limit, it just doesn't make any sense.<br /> <br /> Now perhaps I would make a character that it's a lazzy pants, and thus I would simply not roll if he would require to lift more than 60 (60 being just as much effort as he's willing to make - albeit again, it's an oddly precise limit)...but perhaps the situation changes and he finds the balls to try and lift 80th, and it's going to be much more dramatic that way because it means he managed to pierce his limits both physical and mental because *he cares* (like when a mom lifts a car to save her baby)...instead of being just *what he paid for*</div> </div> </div> </div>You missed the point. Str 2 let you lift max 50kg. Would you tell that your lazy ass str2 mage can only lift 40kg because he is skinny or lazy? Or do you take all what you paid for?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1314950#post1314950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Prime 1 can gauge the power and energy a Wonder has, but not the amount of Effects.<br /> <br /> Prime detects Quintessence, and uses of magick. Wonders have a cost based on their level and the amount of &amp;quot;special properties&amp;quot; (Arete and the optional improvements). A Wonder with a level 5 Effect cost 15 Q, the same as one with a level 3 Effect and Arete 5. The number of Effects <i>per se</i> isn't mentioned as altering the Q cost as far as I can see. Arete rolls DO alter the cost (and define the overal &amp;quot;power&amp;quot; of the Wonder), but because successes are variable (stronger Effects usually require more than weaker ones) you can't know how many Arete rolls and Q went for an certain amount of Effects w/o some serious knowledge of the rules.<br /> <br /> Hence you can't know how much &amp;quot;power&amp;quot; each singular Effect represents inside a Wonder unless you can perfectly issolate each Effect (which, as I've explained, you can't) and thus Prime 1 wouldn't necessarily reveal if the Wonder has more &amp;quot;space&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Where did you got that wonder cost in Q from? The wonder only cost deppending on its effects and they can hold as much as 5 times its lvl, so a lvl 5 can hold 25 Q.<br /> A mage can count effects, as I explained. But even if they didn't, a Prime 1 scan can detect how powerful a spell is, doesn't it? This way the mage can have a notion of how much he can put inside.<br /> <br /> Also, the mage surely knows that a godlike wonder as this (lvl 5) can hold more than 1 power!!! So,it makes no sense for lvl 5 wonders hold just as much as lvl 1 wonders as we see in books.<br /> You can argue against that, even if you consider all my points wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T09:48:55-07:00","id":1315477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You missed the point. Str 2 let you lift max 50kg. Would you tell that your lazy ass str2 mage can only lift 40kg because he is skinny or lazy? Or do you take all what you paid for?</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no problem nerfing myself if there's a roleplaying based reason, but it's hard to imagine a reason to do what you say because of the way Attributes are built.<br /> <br /> a) if you want to be too skinny to lift 40kg, you buy Strength 1. Why leave being skinny to pure interpretation if you have perfectly good rules to model that?<br /> <br /> b) Laziness it's not about being <i>unable </i>to do something, it's about<i> refusing to do it</i> because it requires effort (Carrying a bag?, please, that's what Custos are for...) I wouldn't say 40th is his limit, but I might complain, in character, if he's forced to carry anywhere near that limit, yes.<br /> <br /> A better example, perhaps, would be to buy &quot;free&quot; Defects. It just happens that sometimes the character you want to build would require Defects for a value greater than the maximum of -7. M20 has an allowance to do this and get extra freebies, but the way we did it for Vampire and older Mage (who had no such allowances) was to allow the Defect, but you wouldn't get any more freebies for it. And, personally, I still think that's the best way and the way I would suggest the ST if I needed more Defects to represent a particular character. If you really want that Defect it shouldn't be a problem to get nothing out of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Spheres exist in-game as classifications, created by the hermetics. I know they are not used as paradigms, but there are paradigms that can use this classification. The purple paradigm also is a thing in-game, but few believe in it (mostly the ones that dropped instruments). Paradox and Quintessence are a thing in-game and, while it is not stated that it can be precisely measured (maybe the Etherites did it), it sure can be compared. Mages knows that a sleeper have X ammount of Q and werewolves have 2X. </div> </div> </div>The Purple Pradigm <i>isn't </i>a thing in game. The only reason for anyone to ever mention the PP it's to explain why you can't have it. Even Mages who've dropped their Instruments don't frame their magick in an Arete/Spheres parlance. After all, mages can drop an Instrument from Arete 3 and all mystics can use magick w/o Instruments for a penalt<i>y, it would be stupid to say that all mystic mages believe in the System from the get go, and yet discourage players from ever using such terminology when describing how they make an Effect, don't you think?.</i><br /> <br /> Also note that in M20 mages never surpass their Focus, only their Instruments which it's a <i>part </i>of the Focus. This is intentional - there aren't rules to replace your <i>current Paradigm </i>(that's on your character sheet) with the Purple Paradigm. Hence, no, there's no player character mages with the PP, I would need a very explicit quote from the book saying otherwise to believe the contrary.<br /> <br /> Spheres exist in-game as a 4th wall break, but you still should be able to diferentiate OOC and IOC, ones are a common parlance that doesn't hold enough water to be used as Focus but it's useful to understand other mages, the other are a system to help the players to represent what mages do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, spells and rore are a thing in-game, and they happen to follow the same intention aspect of out-game rules, because they were based of real world &quot;magick&quot;<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> Because that's how they cast spells. A Chorister that prays for his god to burn someone and after the target is burned to wind push knows he had 2 separate outcomes. While if he prays for his target to be burned and pushed (1 pray) he can count it as one outcome.<br /> <br /> Dude, it's quite simple, I don't know how you didn't get it with my A and B scenarios. </div> </div> </div>Because your scenarios don't reflect reality.<br /> <br /> Think about it in D&amp;D 5e terms: Normaly you can only cast one spell per round, but there are some ways to cast two spells on a round (like having a few levels of Figther to use Action Surge). If a wizard (not having read the D&amp;D manual himself) used Action Surge to cast two fireballs on a round you have no problem believing he casted two spells on a round somehow nor you need to compartimentalize this as &quot;one intention&quot; or &quot;two intentions&quot; in order to understand what he's doing. He has a spell called Fireball, and he's casting it twice - NOBODY would ask him how many intentions he had in order to discover if this isn't a new, custom, spell that casts many fireballs instead of one - if anything, a D&amp;D character would pay attention to the material components, the words and the movements used (and the speed of the movements) to determine if the mage used a different spell<br /> <br /> Now my Hermetic can make fireballs, he can cast two fireballs in different turns or in a single turn. If I chose to do so within a 3 seconds interval (but no need of <i>perfect </i>simultaneity, albeit it <i>is </i>happening in a very short amount of time) I need to cast them both as one Effect, while otherwise I could cast them as sepparate Effects, RAW. However, IOC if a D&amp;D wizard saw these fireballs being casted with the same movements, material components, and words being uttered very fast, he would think the mage just casted two spells - two fireballs - on the time frame of a single round.<br /> <br /> Why mages of D&amp;D can frame casting two fireballs on a turn as &quot;casting two spells&quot; but Ascencion mages *need* to frame it as &quot;one spell&quot;? . <i>The only reason to frame Ascencion mage casting two fireballs in one round as &quot;one spell&quot;</i> <b><i>it's rules based</i></b> and, as we agreed before, mages haven't read the book.<br /> <br /> It's never stated that in order to cast two fireballs as a single Effect I necessarily have to frame it as a &quot;one intention&quot; while casting them sligthly slower takes two - it is <i>never </i>mentioned in the rules that my intention to cast two fireballs in a short amount of time has to be realized with with one spell (on the contrary, it's explicitly stated that I can cast two effects in a turn - just not in the sense that the RAW uses when talking about Effects). For my mage these are two Spells! , he casts first spell A and then spell A again - that these are one single Effect it's a requisite of the rules because of how multiple action work in Ascencion, not a requisite of <i>roleplaying</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sorry, I'm not very familiar with that, but Mage spells don't work as genies wishes, you have to be quite direct in what you want. And surely &quot;want to get rich&quot; doesn't work this way. You'd have to be specific of how you want it, so it would be a sequence of spells. </div> </div> </div>Surely the Syndicate thinks it's super complicated and requires a lot of people to do a lot of tasks and a lot of knowledge and Influence, but the fact of the matter it's that getting rich can be acomplished, RAW, as one Effect. See, the system doesn't always reflect the in-game world perfectly<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Where did you got that wonder cost in Q from? The wonder only cost deppending on its effects and they can hold as much as 5 times its lvl, so a lvl 5 can hold 25 Q.<br /> A mage can count effects, as I explained. But even if they didn't, a Prime 1 scan can detect how powerful a spell is, doesn't it? This way the mage can have a notion of how much he can put inside. </div> </div> </div>I was looking at the Wonder creation rules of BoS - And to be frank I think those rules give your point a lot more strenght that what you've just mentioned because they at least allow to diferentiate between lvl 5 Wonders (if not with precision).<br /> <br /> Prime 1 scan can detect the power of a spell, but the power of a spell it's irrelevant to &quot;how much you can put inside&quot; because all the rules say it's that you can place 5 Effects, not how powerful these are (which it's a problem in it's own right, but a different one). Unless mages clearly count their Effects (which isn't the same as counting your spells) knowing how much power a wonder has says nothing to how many more Effects the RAW chosed to allow for OOC balance reasons.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, the mage surely knows that a godlike wonder as this (lvl 5) can hold more than 1 power!!! So,it makes no sense for lvl 5 wonders hold just as much as lvl 1 wonders as we see in books. </div> </div> </div>Nonsense. A Wonder with 5 level 1 Effects it's most likely less godlike than one with 1 lvl 5 Effect (and yet they're of roughly similar &quot;power&quot; from the pov of Prime 1 alone). Level 5 Effects are godlike enough to deserve the Quintessence employed to imbue it in my humble opinion. If that rule allowing more Effects for the same price had never existed nobody would complain, and you wouldn't have existential problems about the canon Wonders.<br /> <br /> In truth, a better, more balanced, system would have in count both the number and the potency of effects to determine the price. What IS nonsense it's a Wonder with 5 lvl 5 Effects costing roughly the same as one with 5 lvl 1, or one with one lvl 5. I'm pretty sure that if you had rules representing all these options with different, balanced, costs you would see no problem in epic Wonders having just one or two Effects (after all, D&amp;D items won't always have more &quot;effects&quot; the more potent they are - sometimes they just have more potent effects).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-30-2019, 01:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T10:33:34-07:00","id":1315496,"title":"","content":"Clarification: the Strength issue is about making a character who can carry somewhere between two door ratings: 40kg is more than what Strength 1 allows, but less than what Strength 2 allows. If, for some reason, you wanted to make a character who could carry more than what one dot allows but less than what two dots allow, the rules don't have the resolution to describe that. So the question is whether you'd take two dots and play the character as only able to lift 40kg (which is more than what one dot allows) or if you'd adjust your concept to let him carry the full 50kg that two dots permit so that you get the full benefit of those two dots. Lord Revan is convinced that you'd do the latter.<br /> <br /> \u201cskinny, but not so skinny as to call for only one dot of Strength\u201d.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-30-2019, 01:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T10:51:52-07:00","id":1315502,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1315496#post1315496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u201cskinny, but not so skinny as to call for only one dot of Strength\u201d.</div> </div> </div> </div>If God opens the sky and tells me &quot;you need to interpret a character that can reliably lift between 30 and 39 kg, but not an ounce more&quot;, I WOULD make one character with Str 2 and then say I can't reliably lift more (if only because making a house rule for what God asks sounds more bother than interpreting it) - I just don't get why this would ever be a thing.<br /> <br /> Honestly, between the Str levels and the fuziness of their limits when you apply Feats, I can't see a player that would seriously find this problem in play. \u201cskinny, but not so skinny as to call for only one dot of Strength&quot; it's not a character to roleplay, it's number crunching to see if the system breaks.<br /> <br /> Make a character that has this exact problem for <i>purely roleplaying reasons </i>and I would both answer <i>yes </i>AND believe that this question it's a real question and not just rethoric to make a point. I think I made abundantly clear my position on the REAL issue when I stated that I would totally build a lvl 5 Wonder with less that 5 effects","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-30-2019, 02:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T11:10:07-07:00","id":1315508,"title":"","content":"It probably wouldn't be. I brought it up initially to point out the principle, that the roleplay-first mindset doesn't automatically try to maximize every benefit that his point purchases grant him. <br /> <br /> Bringing it back to the subject at hand, it's a direct analogy to taking only one Effect in a Wonder when the pricing structure lets you take five for the same cost. In both cases, the concept-first mindset doesn't have a problem with taking less than what the rules offer, if that's what the concept calls for. By contrast, the rules-first mindset has difficulty accepting that there's ever a valid reason to do it.<br /> <br /> The difference in the two cases is that in the Strength case, it's hard to come up with a concept that calls for an intermediate Strength; with the Wonder case, it's quite easy to do so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-30-2019, 02:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T11:18:17-07:00","id":1315513,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that. I'm sorry if I'm a bit salty about that example.<br /> <br /> It doesn't sound like a question that makes sense to ask after so many people already told you, and abundantly, that &quot;the roleplay-first mindset doesn't automatically try to maximize every benefit that his point purchases grant him&quot;. I can't but ask &quot;why is Revan so insistent with this after the question was clearly answered&quot;?. It rustless my jimmies","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T11:27:28-07:00","id":1315517,"title":"","content":"Firsthand experience from two decades when I personally had the rules-first mentality myself: it's a blindspot. When you're used to thinking in terms of rules first and concept second, it's very common to be unable to wrap your head around the notion that there's any other way to do it. Even when people tell you otherwise, your instinctive reaction is to dismiss their claims out of hand, or to think that they're being stupid.<br /> <br /> And the reason why I said that this issue needs to be addressed first is that Lord Revan is protecting a version of the same mindset onto the mages themselves: just like he can't see why a player wouldn't take advantage of every optimization available, he also can't see why a mage wouldn't do likewise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-30-2019, 02:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T12:16:05-07:00","id":1315536,"title":"","content":"Ok.<br /> <br /> Then the answer is <i>yes,</i> I would tell the group that my character ain't having the full benefits of Str 2 if it was important to get to that level of detail for some reason, and make a note.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T16:41:35-07:00","id":1315598,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1315477#post1315477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Purple Pradigm <i>isn't </i>a thing in game. The only reason for anyone to ever mention the PP it's to explain why you can't have it. Even Mages who've dropped their Instruments don't frame their magick in an Arete/Spheres parlance. After all, mages can drop an Instrument from Arete 3 and all mystics can use magick w/o Instruments for a penalt<i>y, it would be stupid to say that all mystic mages believe in the System from the get go, and yet discourage players from ever using such terminology when describing how they make an Effect, don't you think?.</i><br /> <br /> Also note that in M20 mages never surpass their Focus, only their Instruments which it's a <i>part </i>of the Focus. This is intentional - there aren't rules to replace your <i>current Paradigm </i>(that's on your character sheet) with the Purple Paradigm. Hence, no, there's no player character mages with the PP, I would need a very explicit quote from the book saying otherwise to believe the contrary.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just a little observations here. The Purle Paradigm is a thing <b>in-game</b>. I mean, it is in-game the reason why magick works (enlightened Will). You are right about the rest. And this doens't make a difference in our discussion, though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1315477#post1315477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because your scenarios don't reflect reality.<br /> <br /> Think about it in D&amp;D 5e terms: Normaly you can only cast one spell per round, but there are some ways to cast two spells on a round (like having a few levels of Figther to use Action Surge). If a wizard (not having read the D&amp;D manual himself) used Action Surge to cast two fireballs on a round you have no problem believing he casted two spells on a round somehow nor you need to compartimentalize this as &quot;one intention&quot; or &quot;two intentions&quot; in order to understand what he's doing. He has a spell called Fireball, and he's casting it twice - NOBODY would ask him how many intentions he had in order to discover if this isn't a new, custom, spell that casts many fireballs instead of one - if anything, a D&amp;D character would pay attention to the material components, the words and the movements used (and the speed of the movements) to determine if the mage used a different spell<br /> <br /> Now my Hermetic can make fireballs, he can cast two fireballs in different turns or in a single turn. If I chose to do so within a 3 seconds interval (but no need of <i>perfect </i>simultaneity, albeit it <i>is </i>happening in a very short amount of time) I need to cast them both as one Effect, while otherwise I could cast them as sepparate Effects, RAW. However, IOC if a D&amp;D wizard saw these fireballs being casted with the same movements, material components, and words being uttered very fast, he would think the mage just casted two spells - two fireballs - on the time frame of a single round.<br /> <br /> Why mages of D&amp;D can frame casting two fireballs on a turn as &quot;casting two spells&quot; but Ascencion mages *need* to frame it as &quot;one spell&quot;? . <i>The only reason to frame Ascencion mage casting two fireballs in one round as &quot;one spell&quot;</i> <b><i>it's rules based</i></b> and, as we agreed before, mages haven't read the book.<br /> <br /> It's never stated that in order to cast two fireballs as a single Effect I necessarily have to frame it as a &quot;one intention&quot; while casting them sligthly slower takes two - it is <i>never </i>mentioned in the rules that my intention to cast two fireballs in a short amount of time has to be realized with with one spell (on the contrary, it's explicitly stated that I can cast two effects in a turn - just not in the sense that the RAW uses when talking about Effects). For my mage these are two Spells! , he casts first spell A and then spell A again - that these are one single Effect it's a requisite of the rules because of how multiple action work in Ascencion, not a requisite of <i>roleplaying</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, I stopped at D&amp;D 3.5 (the best version IMHO, but I didn't get to know much about the others XD). <br /> Let me simplify even more. Forget about turns. Forget about casting time and 3 seconds. Forget how many fireballs are comming out of his hand.<br /> You example mage that casted two fireballs: How many times did he had to use his instruments?<br /> <br /> If you answers &quot;1&quot;, then it is one spell, rote, effect, whatever. If you answers &quot;2&quot;, then it is two whatevers being casted. The mage won't count how many balls are exploding or how many time it took. In fact the mage could make an effect \u00e0 la &quot;Magic Missile&quot; and one simple spell casting 5 (or evendozens) of little missiles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1315477#post1315477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nonsense. A Wonder with 5 level 1 Effects it's most likely less godlike than one with 1 lvl 5 Effect (and yet they're of roughly similar &quot;power&quot; from the pov of Prime 1 alone). Level 5 Effects are godlike enough to deserve the Quintessence employed to imbue it in my humble opinion. If that rule allowing more Effects for the same price had never existed nobody would complain, and you wouldn't have existential problems about the canon Wonders.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think they would be counted similarly with Prime 1. A lvl 5 effects is waaay more powerfull than 5 lvl 1. This certainly would be felt by the mage.<br /> <br /> And you missed my point. The wonder creator knows that more potent wonders can hold more spells. If he is creating a very potent one, it certainly can hold more than that single spell, even that he doesn't know how many it can hold (which I believe he should know). So, still there is no explanation for lvl 5 wonders with just one spell in-game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1315477#post1315477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In truth, a better, more balanced, system would have in count both the number and the potency of effects to determine the price. What IS nonsense it's a Wonder with 5 lvl 5 Effects costing roughly the same as one with 5 lvl 1, or one with one lvl 5. I'm pretty sure that if you had rules representing all these options with different, balanced, costs you would see no problem in epic Wonders having just one or two Effects (after all, D&amp;D items won't always have more &quot;effects&quot; the more potent they are - sometimes they just have more potent effects).</div> </div> </div> </div>Totally agree.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-07T07:37:15-07:00","id":1316985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1315598#post1315598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a little observations here. The Purle Paradigm is a thing <b>in-game</b>. I mean, it is in-game the reason why magick works (enlightened Will). You are right about the rest. And this doens't make a difference in our discussion, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>The term &quot;Purple Paradigm&quot; was invented by the comunity to call out when your Paradigm it's efectively The Rules with a thin varnish. It was called like that because the Mage Core cover it's purple<i>. It's a terminology that was invented to tell you &quot;no, you can't have that Paradigm&quot;</i><br /> <br /> I would need an <i>explicit </i>quote (either IOC or stating that mages know this IOC) to accept it exists in-game now. I made a search on M20 books and found no mention of &quot;purple paradigm&quot;. To say something that's not even mentioned in the books it's &quot;the true reality&quot; sounds a bit cheap to me.<br /> <br /> You would have to elaborate this point. Perhaps you mean that <i>part </i>of what we call the PP it's the true reality. That could be more debatable depending on what is that part you want to defend.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You example mage that casted two fireballs: How many times did he had to use his instruments?<br /> <br /> If you answers &quot;1&quot;, then it is one spell, rote, effect, whatever. If you answers &quot;2&quot;, then it is two whatevers being casted. The mage won't count how many balls are exploding or how many time it took. In fact the mage could make an effect \u00e0 la &quot;Magic Missile&quot; and one simple spell casting 5 (or evendozens) of little missiles. </div> </div> </div>I used my Instrument TWO (2) times in a single turn to cast ONE single Effect.<br /> <br /> Or I'm supposed to believe MtA Mages can't hurl two material components in a 3 seconds lapse?. There's nothing in the rules saying I can't use my Instrument twice in a turn, because Instrument usage it's pure interpretation.<br /> <br /> Let me give you a clear example: My Etherite has two ray-guns invented as Instruments to cast two different effects (a freezing ray and a heat ray) - this is 100% canon, you may not like it but ray-guns can used as &quot;Device/Machine&quot; instruments.<br /> <br /> Now, he's in a figth with two oponents<br /> <br /> OOC: I want to hit both in a turn because I want to end this quick and I have enough Arete to kill two people in a row. But I decided my rayguns can't shoot more than one ray each (that's the way I've been using them from the first time). Hence I ask the ST to cast an improvised Conjunctional Effect: A ray of heat and a ray of ice are going to hit my enemies this turn, and to cast them I declare <i>both </i>weapons as my Instrument (thematically appropriate, and allowed by the rules. You can cast one Effect with multiple Instruments, remember?, and some Instruments are composed by more than one discrete object).<br /> <br /> IOC He lifts his heat gun and blast opponent A with a heat ray. Not wanting to give his other opponent the chance of retaliate, in a split of a second he lifts the freezing gun and freezes the bastard.<br /> <br /> OOC it's one Effect using Violence as a Focus, but I need to divide my dicepool between <i>two </i>attacks (I AM shooting two guns)<br /> <br /> IOC it's two energy blasters firing two energy blasts.<br /> <br /> If I had done that in two turns instead of one it would be two Effects OOC, and still two energy blasters firing two energy blasts IOC.<br /> <br /> It's ludicrous to say that shooting two guns in 3 seconds it's clearly one Instrument IOC, but doing exactly the same thing sligthly slower it's clearly two Instruments (or &quot;two uses&quot; vs &quot;one use&quot;). Even if the rules are clear about that<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think they would be counted similarly with Prime 1. A lvl 5 effects is waaay more powerfull than 5 lvl 1. This certainly would be felt by the mage. </div> </div> </div>That's very relative, a one success lvl 5 Effect may be weaker in many senses than a lvl 1 Effect with 100 successes behind (it surely would be easier to dispell, for instance). A Level 5 Wonder requires roughly the same successes for 5 level 1 effects than it would for<br /> <br /> Also the Quintessence involved it's the same, and that's what Prime 1 detects first and foremost. I bet it can also detect the amount of successes because Prime it's important to Countermagick and Unweaving...but Sphere level, nope, Prime 1 doesn't detect that, not even roughly<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And you missed my point. The wonder creator knows that more potent wonders can hold more spells. If he is creating a very potent one, it certainly can hold more than that single spell, even that he doesn't know how many it can hold (which I believe he should know). So, still there is no explanation for lvl 5 wonders with just one spell in-game. </div> </div> </div>A Wonder with one lvl 5 Effect can, potentially, cost the same amount of Quintessence, and successes (IOC: &quot;Magical Power&quot;) than one with other 4 Effects - additional features like Merits muddle the waters even more becuase they cost Quint and Successes. My argument didn't miss yours because you would need to be able to determine <i>not </i>how powerful it's a wonder,<i> but how powerful it's a wonder with one effect vs one with five</i>...and that's not really detectable trough Prime 1.<br /> <br /> Or rather not with presicion. If you were to experiment a <i>long</i> time, and the Rules mirrored the world perfectly, you would eventually notice a trend on Quintessence expenditure and &quot;effort&quot;. Statician mages would be able to pinpoint these with accuracy. But you're bound to discover the Spheres and Arete a loot sooner than that if you can do this<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> I think we've debated this long enough to admit, at least, a <i>plausible deniability </i>on mages <i>knowing </i>that they're wasting power when they don't make a Wonder with as much effects as the Rules allow as long as the Rules aren't a 1:1 reflection of the world but rather an imprecise generalization. Don't you think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 06-07-2019, 12:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-08T12:32:50-07:00","id":1317223,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1316985#post1316985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The term &quot;Purple Paradigm&quot; was invented by the comunity to call out when your Paradigm it's efectively The Rules with a thin varnish. It was called like that because the Mage Core cover it's purple<i>. It's a terminology that was invented to tell you &quot;no, you can't have that Paradigm&quot;</i><br /> <br /> I would need an <i>explicit </i>quote (either IOC or stating that mages know this IOC) to accept it exists in-game now. I made a search on M20 books and found no mention of &quot;purple paradigm&quot;. To say something that's not even mentioned in the books it's &quot;the true reality&quot; sounds a bit cheap to me.<br /> <br /> You would have to elaborate this point. Perhaps you mean that <i>part </i>of what we call the PP it's the true reality. That could be more debatable depending on what is that part you want to defend.</div> </div> </div> </div>I will elaborate, since you didn't get what I was saying. My point is that in-game, magick works because mages Will it so. That's it. And that <b>is</b> the purple paradigm. I never said that it is a paradigm that can be taken by a mage (altough if the ST allows I don't see why not) nor that we have examples of it. I'd say that a master that already dropped all his instruments <b>might</b>, I said might, understand that this is the true reality (in-game) and start to change his paradigm to it. We both know that dropping instruments doesn't mean dropping focus, so this is entirelly up to ST.<br /> <br /> But it is just that. I just said that in-game magick works because of Awakened Will. And that is a fact. Pointed over and over in the books. I don't even remember why we brought this into the discussion, so we might as well stop right here.<br /> <br /> Having said that, I find intersting that there are some paradigms very similar to the &quot;Purple paradigm&quot;, like &quot;Everything is an Illusion&quot; or &quot;Everything is Chaos&quot;, where the mage believe that reality is in fact what our mind believe it is. This is very close to magick being a result of Will, so I don't see much problem if a ST allow someone to take the Purple Paradigm.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1316985#post1316985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I used my Instrument TWO (2) times in a single turn to cast ONE single Effect.<br /> <br /> Or I'm supposed to believe MtA Mages can't hurl two material components in a 3 seconds lapse?. There's nothing in the rules saying I can't use my Instrument twice in a turn, because Instrument usage it's pure interpretation.<br /> <br /> Let me give you a clear example: My Etherite has two ray-guns invented as Instruments to cast two different effects (a freezing ray and a heat ray) - this is 100% canon, you may not like it but ray-guns can used as &quot;Device/Machine&quot; instruments.<br /> <br /> Now, he's in a figth with two oponents<br /> <br /> OOC: I want to hit both in a turn because I want to end this quick and I have enough Arete to kill two people in a row. But I decided my rayguns can't shoot more than one ray each (that's the way I've been using them from the first time). Hence I ask the ST to cast an improvised Conjunctional Effect: A ray of heat and a ray of ice are going to hit my enemies this turn, and to cast them I declare <i>both </i>weapons as my Instrument (thematically appropriate, and allowed by the rules. You can cast one Effect with multiple Instruments, remember?, and some Instruments are composed by more than one discrete object).<br /> <br /> IOC He lifts his heat gun and blast opponent A with a heat ray. Not wanting to give his other opponent the chance of retaliate, in a split of a second he lifts the freezing gun and freezes the bastard.<br /> <br /> OOC it's one Effect using Violence as a Focus, but I need to divide my dicepool between <i>two </i>attacks (I AM shooting two guns)<br /> <br /> IOC it's two energy blasters firing two energy blasts.<br /> <br /> If I had done that in two turns instead of one it would be two Effects OOC, and still two energy blasters firing two energy blasts IOC.<br /> <br /> It's ludicrous to say that shooting two guns in 3 seconds it's clearly one Instrument IOC, but doing exactly the same thing sligthly slower it's clearly two Instruments (or &quot;two uses&quot; vs &quot;one use&quot;). Even if the rules are clear about that</div> </div> </div> </div>You are going too far to make you argument right. It's a simple fact that when creating a wonder, the mage will cast a single spell to do a single effect and if he wants more effects, he would just cast more.<br /> But it's ok, I'll play along.<br /> <br /> You use your instrument to cast an effect. We have three possible effects: shooting a heat ray, shooting a freezing ray and shooting a combined heat/freezing ray. If you say that to shoot a freezing/heat ray you somehow combine both guns into one big gun \u00e0 La Power Rangers and shoot one ray, that's ok. If you want just to shoot heat or freezing ray with your heat/freeze gun respectively, that's ok.<br /> Now, I wouldn't allow using two guns that does two different effect in one round. In your case you are trying to mix them into a single effect, and like I said unless you somehow activate them at the same time, I'd say you can't do that.<br /> But, as you tried to mix them into one single effect, I see no reason to house rule and allow such thing, since it is not something that is going to ruin the fun of the game.<br /> <br /> As default, each gun cast one effect, and only one effect can be cast in a turn no matter what, so I'd say your example is at least very, very strange and I never seen such thing anywhere as using two rolls to hit in a single effect. What if the first one hits and the second doesn't? You get half the damage? There are no rules for this, so it's a house-rule.<br /> <br /> Also, your example is an exception. All the examples we see in the books are straightforward and simple as &quot;mage wants to cast a spell, uses his instruments, spell is cast&quot;. Doing just that, you indeed <b>can</b> count how many spells you are casting on a wonder.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1316985#post1316985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's very relative, a one success lvl 5 Effect may be weaker in many senses than a lvl 1 Effect with 100 successes behind (it surely would be easier to dispell, for instance). A Level 5 Wonder requires roughly the same successes for 5 level 1 effects than it would for<br /> <br /> Also the Quintessence involved it's the same, and that's what Prime 1 detects first and foremost. I bet it can also detect the amount of successes because Prime it's important to Countermagick and Unweaving...but Sphere level, nope, Prime 1 doesn't detect that, not even roughly<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A Wonder with one lvl 5 Effect can, potentially, cost the same amount of Quintessence, and successes (IOC: &quot;Magical Power&quot;) than one with other 4 Effects - additional features like Merits muddle the waters even more becuase they cost Quint and Successes. My argument didn't miss yours because you would need to be able to determine <i>not </i>how powerful it's a wonder,<i> but how powerful it's a wonder with one effect vs one with five</i>...and that's not really detectable trough Prime 1.<br /> <br /> Or rather not with presicion. If you were to experiment a <i>long</i> time, and the Rules mirrored the world perfectly, you would eventually notice a trend on Quintessence expenditure and &quot;effort&quot;. Statician mages would be able to pinpoint these with accuracy. But you're bound to discover the Spheres and Arete a loot sooner than that if you can do this</div> </div> </div> </div>Successe has nothing to do with the power behind an effect. It just means how well done it is made. Otherwise you wouldn't require Forces 5 to cast a blizzard, only Forces 1 and 100 successes. Your logic is flawed. If the wonder is capable of casting a tornado, the mage knows that it is made with a high powered spell. If the wonder hasa simple, but well made effect, it means that it's a low level power with a lot of successes. Also, there is a number of successes that begins to not make any difference. If to create a sunglass that allows you to see other color spectrum it doens't matter if you got 5 or 100 successes, the result is the same. <br /> <br /> So, with Prime 1 the mage indeed <b>is</b> able to feel the powerlevel of an effect or a wonder's effect. He might not be able to differentiate with precision, but he certainly can feel the difference between a lvl 1 and a lvl 5 power.<br /> <br /> Hell, even if you doesn't have Prime or isn't a mage at all!! Using logic you can see the power level of a wonder. A wonder that steps you out of time is certainly waaay more powerfull than a wonder that creates a little light. Anyone is capable to use logic and know at least how to differentiate a lame ass lvl 1 wonder from an all-powerfull master wonder. And if the mage is creating a all-powerfull master wonder, he knows that he can make it able to do more than one thing (not even considering effects, anymore, but output of the wonder).<br /> <br /> Let me give you this example: A wonder costs 5 Quintessence per dot. So, a 5 dot wonder costs 25 Q. So, a Master crafts two 5 dot wonders. The first uses 25 Q (let's say a whole werewolf body blood) and has one power like stepping out of time. The second, also costs 25Q (also a werewolf full body blood) and steps you out of time, freezes time and spees you up (giving extra actions).<br /> <br /> In this case, anyone, I mean anyone: even a sleeper friend of the mage with no knowledge of Arete, spheres, effect, spells, anything, could just ask the creator: &quot;Dude, with the same ammount of material you created the second wonder waaay more powerfull than the first one.Why is that?&quot;. And then the mage will think, that all he had to do was put two more spells inside the first wonder during creation, just like he did in the second one. Doesn't matter how his focus works, if he can count spells or intentions. All that matters is that he knows that he can choose the output of the wonder powers.<br /> <br /> Of course, if the mage is inexperient, or if it's the first wonder that he's creating, you could argue that he didn't know that a wonder would support more than 1 power output. But we are talking about Masters here, that created dozens of wonders, some with more than 1 power, others with just 1. If you already created mid level wonders with more than one power, what would make you believe that you cannot create a master piece wonder with more than 1??<br /> <br /> Again, forget the counting effects or intents thing. Just counting the output powers is enough.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1310071-custom-wonders?p=1316985#post1316985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think we've debated this long enough to admit, at least, a <i>plausible deniability </i>on mages <i>knowing </i>that they're wasting power when they don't make a Wonder with as much effects as the Rules allow as long as the Rules aren't a 1:1 reflection of the world but rather an imprecise generalization. Don't you think?</div> </div> </div> </div>As just said above, even that there is no 1:1 reflection, any mage that is just a little experienced in creating wonders, as a Master of Prime probably is, should be able to know that he indeed can put more than 1 power in a wonder. He might not know that he can put 5 effects in a lvl 5 and 3 in a lvl 3, but at least more than 1 in a lvl 5 wonder..<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}